# Tiny Pointers — arXiv:2111.12800 (Bender, Conway, Farach-Colton, Kuszmaul,
# Tagliavini) — implemented in Flow.
#
# A dereference table lets n "users" (keys) each own one slot in a shared
# value array. A naive implementation hands each user a log n-bit pointer.
# The paper's key insight: because the key and the pointer are *both* known
# at dereference time, the pointer itself only needs to encode the slot
# within a hash-derived bucket — o(log n) bits total.
#
# This example implements the paper's optimal FIXED-SIZE construction
# (Theorem 1, size O(log log log n + log δ^-1)):
#
#   Primary table   — a load-balancing table: hash the key into one of
#                     m/b buckets of b slots, return the slot index. Fails
#                     (overflows) on a ~δ² fraction of allocations.
#   Secondary table — a power-of-two-choices table at sparse load factor:
#                     hash into TWO buckets, place in the one with more
#                     free slots; the pointer stores 1 bit of choice + the
#                     slot. Absorbs the primary's overflow w.h.p.
#   Tiny pointer    — 1 bit (which table) + log b bits (slot in bucket).
#
# Concrete instance: n = 65536 slots (log n = 16 bits), δ = 1/4, so
# b = 32 for the primary and b = 8 for the secondary:
#
#     tiny pointer = 6 bits   vs   raw pointer = 16 bits   (62% fewer bits)
#
# On top of the dereference table we implement the paper's THIRD
# application — a space-efficient STABLE dictionary (Theorem 8): the
# dictionary's value array is replaced by an array of tiny pointers into
# the dereference table, so a value's position never changes after insert
# (referential integrity), at O(log v) extra bits per value.
#
# Phase 5-6 implement the paper's VARIABLE-SIZE construction (Theorem 2,
# Section 4): a recursive container/level table with overflow arrays that
# hands out O(1 + log δ^-1) EXPECTED-bit pointers whose size tail is DOUBLY
# exponential (Pr[size ≥ ℓ] ≤ 2^(−2^Ω(ℓ))). Phase 5 loads 49152 keys
# naturally (~98% get 3-bit pointers); Phase 6 forces an adversarial
# cascade of 20 keys into one container (found by offline bit-exact hash
# search) that drills to level 3 and engages the overflow arrays — and
# still never exceeds an 11-bit pointer. The empirical size distribution
# is checked against the theoretical tail bound.
#
# Phase 7-8 implement the paper's FIRST application — RELAXED RETRIEVAL
# (Theorem 6, Section 6.2). Classic dynamic retrieval provably costs
# Ω(log log n) metadata bits per value. Relax the contract: Insert(x, y)
# returns an O(1)-expected hint r the user keeps, and Query(x, r) / Delete(x, r)
# present both. The hint IS a variable-size tiny pointer; Dereference(x, r)
# names a slot unique to x, and the value lives in a hash table H keyed on
# that slot. H's keys come from the tiny universe [2n], so H costs nv + O(n)
# bits (log C(2n,n) = O(n) by Stirling). Phase 7 measures the hint-size
# distribution against the Ω(log log n) lower bound; Phase 8 churns with
# tombstoned deletes, forces adversarial hints, and rebuilds H — all hints
# survive untouched. Phase 8b sweeps the THEOREM 6 TRADEOFF: r = 1..4 levels
# of indirection in the dereference table give O(r)-time insertions/deletions
# against nv + O(n log⁽ʳ⁾ n) bits — the retriever (level-0 slot code) costs
# w₀ = ⌈log⁽ʳ⁾ n⌉ bits (15 → 4 → 2 → 1 at n = 2^14) while the worst-case
# cascade descends all r levels.
#
# Phase 9-10 implement the paper's SECOND application — SUCCINCT ROTATION-
# BASED BINARY SEARCH TREES (Theorem 7, Section 6.3). Each node stores its
# key/value plus two tiny retrievers (r₁ for the left child keyed on x◦0, r₂
# for the right child keyed on x◦1); the dereference table's store IS the node
# array, so a child is read in O(1) by dereferencing the retriever. A rotation
# rewrites only 2-3 store slots — nodes and retrievers never move — so the
# structure stays nk + O(n) bits while rotations remain constant-time.
# Phase 9 builds an AVL tree from 16384 SORTED keys (maximal rotations) and
# verifies order, balance, and that every retriever/slot/position is intact;
# Phase 10 storms 20000 random rotations and shows in-order order is preserved
# with zero pointer movement.
#
# Phase 11 implements the paper's FOURTH application — VARIABLE-SIZE VALUE
# DICTIONARIES (Theorem 9, Section 6.5). Any dictionary designed for uniform-
# size values can store arbitrary-length values (up to O(1) machine words):
# each v-bit value lives in size class ⌈log₂ v⌉, whose slots are exactly
# 2^⌈log v⌉ bits, so the value space is Σ(2^⌈log vᵢ⌉ + O(log vᵢ)) instead of
# a uniform 64-bit slot per key. Phase 11 loads 49152 keys with a skewed size
# distribution (70% ≤ 8 bits), verifies every value bit-exactly, churns, and
# adds a uniform 64-bit control that shows the bound becoming tight.
#
# Phase 12 applies Theorem 9's remark ('the same techniques work … without
# modification') to the KEY ARRAY: size-class the keys exactly like the values,
# so a k-bit key costs 2^⌈log k⌉ + O(log k) bits instead of a uniform 64-bit
# slot. Phase 12 stores BOTH variable-size keys and variable-size values per
# pair and measures the COMBINED key+value space against a uniform 64+64-bit
# baseline.
#
# Phase 12 also lifts the KEY cap exactly as Phase 15 lifts the VALUE cap: the
# key side runs the same r-levels-of-indirection chain, so 128/256-bit keys
# live in a compact key arena behind a 5-bit p₁ held in an 8-bit base holder
# slot (flag bit 7 = chained key), and the base pointer stays 8 bits for every
# key size — the key analogue of Phase 15's base-pointer independence. The
# measured combined space is reported against BOTH uniform baselines: 64+64
# (the old caps) and 256+64 (the new caps).
#
# Phase 13 demonstrates the paper's Section-5 lower bounds (Theorems 3-5)
# empirically on the live tables: the fullest-bin floor F(s)·2^s ≥ log₂log₂m
# holds at every pointer budget s, the variable-size mean sits at/above
# log₂δ⁻¹, and masking the 6-bit pointer below its real width forces
# dereference collisions (a scheme cannot spend fewer bits than the floor).
#
# Phase 14 implements the paper's zone-aggregated DEAMORTIZED dynamic resizing
# (Section 6.1, part of Theorem 9's proof): the class storage grows/shrinks
# incrementally at a fixed per-op budget (RV_BUDGET = 16 values migrated +
# RV_SCAN = 64 registry entries per op) with an epoch cursor and a second,
# destination generation — the worst single op drops from 143,363 work units
# (the naive Θ(live) trigger-op copy) to 81.
#
# Phase 15 implements the paper's r-LEVELS OF INDIRECTION chain (Theorem 9's
# proof, Section 6.5): a k-bit value is stored at the end of a linked list —
# 'value k bits ← p₁ of O(log k) bits ← … ← base pointer of O(log⁽ʳ⁾ n) bits' —
# lifting the v ≤ 64 cap. 128/256-bit values live in a level-1 arena behind a
# 5-bit p₁ held in an 8-bit base slot, and the base pointer stays 8 bits for
# EVERY value size (the paper's base-pointer independence). Space is measured
# against the single-level size-classed table and a uniform 256-bit baseline.
#
# Phase 15 (r = 3 extension) inserts the paper's O(log log k) MIDDLE pointer:
# values up to 2^32 bits flow through base(8 bits) → p₂(6 bits) → p₁(5 bits)
# → value — a three-level chain. The value words live in a shared pool behind
# the 5-bit p₁; p₁ lives in a level-2 table behind the 6-bit p₂; the 8-bit
# base slot holds p₂. Every level's pointer stays CONSTANT as v grows — the
# base never changes from its 8 bits — and the chain's per-big-value overhead
# rises from 13 bits (r=2: 8-bit holder + 5-bit p₁) to 19 bits (r=3: 8-bit
# holder + 6-bit p₂ + 5-bit p₁) while the value cap lifts from 256 bits to
# 2^32 bits.
#
# Phase 16 implements the paper's FINAL application — the OPTIMAL INTERNAL-
# MEMORY STASH (Theorem 10, Section 6.6). External memory is a dereference
# table with load factor 1 − ε (ε = 3/4): ST_NB buckets × ST_B slots, and each
# key's bucket is IMPLICIT — a pure two-choice hash of the key. The internal
# stash therefore stores only the intra-bucket address per key: 1 choice bit +
# log₂(ST_B) slot bits = a 4-bit tiny pointer = Θ(log ε⁻¹), never
# log₂((1+ε)m) = 15 bits (the naive full-address stash). Stash lookup is the
# adaptive filter of Bender et al.: keys are binned by their hash quotient
# (the bin index IS the quotient — stored nowhere), every entry carries a
# PREFIX-FREE fingerprint whose length grows only enough to separate bin-mates
# (the paper's adaptivity bits, O(m) total), and Query(k) finds its entry in
# O(1) expected RAM steps then reads external memory EXACTLY ONCE. Inserts and
# deletes are O(1) expected; positions are permanent (page-table stability);
# the stash is O(m log ε⁻¹) = O(m) bits, measured against the naive stash and
# the paper's bound.
#
# Everything is self-verifying: every allocation is checked by re-running
# Dereference(key, pointer) and comparing against the recorded position and
# value. Run with:  FLOW_HOST=python ./flow run examples/systems/tiny_pointers.flow

extern {
    function malloc(size: i64) -> ptr<u8>
    function free(p: ptr<u8>)
    function flow_rt_monotonic_ns() -> i64
    function printf(fmt: string, val: f64) -> i32
}

# Wall-clock (monotonic) nanosecond counter — powers the benchmark block.
function now_ns() -> i64 {
    return flow_rt_monotonic_ns()
}

# Milliseconds from a stored nanosecond duration (f64, for printf("%.2f", ...)).
function elapsed_ms(d_ns: i64) -> f64 {
    return (d_ns as f64) / 1000000.0
}

# ============================================================================
# Constants
# ============================================================================

const N_SLOTS: i32 = 65536        # total slots in the dereference table
const LOG_N: i32 = 16             # bits in a raw pointer (log2 N_SLOTS)
const DELTA_NUM: i32 = 1          # δ = 1/4
const DELTA_DEN: i32 = 4
const MAX_LIVE: i32 = 49152       # (1 - δ)·n — load factor 0.75

const P_SLOTS: i32 = 57344        # primary size  (1 - δ/2)·n
const P_BUCKET: i32 = 32          # b_primary = Θ(δ^-2 log δ^-1) ≈ 32
const P_BUCKETS: i32 = 1792       # P_SLOTS / P_BUCKET

const S_SLOTS: i32 = 8192         # secondary size  δ·n/2
const S_BUCKET: i32 = 8           # b_secondary = Θ(log log n) ≈ 8
const S_BUCKETS: i32 = 1024       # S_SLOTS / S_BUCKET

const TP_TABLE_BIT: i32 = 32      # bit 5 of the tiny pointer: 0=primary 1=secondary
const TP_BITS: i32 = 6            # fixed tiny-pointer size in bits

const REG_SIZE: i32 = 131072      # registry of all keys ever inserted
const DMAP_CAP: i32 = 131072      # open-addressing map capacity (dictionary demo)
const KEY_MASK: i32 = 16777215    # keys live in [0, 2^24)

# ============================================================================
# Primitives
# ============================================================================

# Murmur-style finalizer, salted for independence (h1, h2, map probes...).
function hash_u32(x: i32, salt: i32) -> i32 {
    let mut h: i32 = x ^ (salt * 2654435761)
    h = ((h >> 16) ^ h) * 0x45d9f3b
    h = ((h >> 16) ^ h) * 0x45d9f3b
    h = (h >> 16) ^ h
    if h < 0 {
        h = 0 - h
    }
    return h
}

# xorshift32 PRNG. Deterministic per seed, so the whole demo is reproducible.
function rng_next(state: ptr<i32>) -> i32 {
    let mut x: i32 = state[0]
    if x == 0 {
        x = 2463534242
    }
    x = x ^ (x << 13)
    x = x ^ (x >> 17)
    x = x ^ (x << 5)
    state[0] = x
    if x < 0 {
        x = 0 - x
    }
    return x
}

# Distinct keys via modular multiplication: key_i = i·11469 mod 2^24.
# 11469 is odd, so this cycles through all 2^24 values without repetition.
function distinct_key(i: i32) -> i32 {
    return (i * 11469) & KEY_MASK
}

# ============================================================================
# Dereference table
# ============================================================================

struct PrimaryTable {
    counts: ptr<i32>,   # P_BUCKETS — occupied count per bucket
    bitmap: ptr<i32>    # P_BUCKETS — one 32-bit word per bucket
}

struct SecondaryTable {
    counts: ptr<i32>,   # S_BUCKETS
    bitmap: ptr<i32>    # S_BUCKETS
}

function primary_create() -> PrimaryTable {
    let raw_c: ptr<u8> = malloc(P_BUCKETS * 4)
    let raw_b: ptr<u8> = malloc(P_BUCKETS * 4)
    let counts: ptr<i32> = raw_c
    let bitmap: ptr<i32> = raw_b
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < P_BUCKETS {
        counts[i] = 0
        bitmap[i] = 0
        i = i + 1
    }
    return PrimaryTable { counts: counts, bitmap: bitmap }
}

function secondary_create() -> SecondaryTable {
    let raw_c: ptr<u8> = malloc(S_BUCKETS * 4)
    let raw_b: ptr<u8> = malloc(S_BUCKETS * 4)
    let counts: ptr<i32> = raw_c
    let bitmap: ptr<i32> = raw_b
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < S_BUCKETS {
        counts[i] = 0
        bitmap[i] = 0
        i = i + 1
    }
    return SecondaryTable { counts: counts, bitmap: bitmap }
}

function primary_destroy(pr: PrimaryTable) {
    let raw_c: ptr<u8> = pr.counts
    let raw_b: ptr<u8> = pr.bitmap
    free(raw_c)
    free(raw_b)
}

function secondary_destroy(sec: SecondaryTable) {
    let raw_c: ptr<u8> = sec.counts
    let raw_b: ptr<u8> = sec.bitmap
    free(raw_c)
    free(raw_b)
}

# Lowest 0 bit in the low `limit` bits of a bucket bitmap; -1 if none.
function find_free_bit(bm: i32, limit: i32) -> i32 {
    let mut s: i32 = 0
    while s < limit {
        let bit: i32 = (bm >> s) & 1
        if bit == 0 {
            return s
        }
        s = s + 1
    }
    return -1
}

# Allocate a slot for `key`, store `value` there. Returns the tiny pointer
# (>= 0), or -1 if the whole table is over capacity (should not happen at
# load factor 1 - δ). Writes the absolute slot index to pos_out for the
# caller to verify against Dereference later.
function tp_allocate(pr: PrimaryTable, sec: SecondaryTable, values: ptr<i32>,
                     key: i32, value: i32, pos_out: ptr<i32>) -> i32 {
    let b: i32 = hash_u32(key, 1) % P_BUCKETS
    let slot: i32 = find_free_bit(pr.bitmap[b], P_BUCKET)
    if slot >= 0 {
        pr.bitmap[b] = pr.bitmap[b] | (1 << slot)
        pr.counts[b] = pr.counts[b] + 1
        let pos: i32 = b * P_BUCKET + slot
        values[pos] = value
        pos_out[0] = pos
        return slot                    # tiny pointer: table bit 0 + slot
    }
    # Primary bucket full → power-of-two-choices into the secondary.
    let b1: i32 = hash_u32(key, 2) % S_BUCKETS
    let b2: i32 = hash_u32(key, 3) % S_BUCKETS
    let mut pick: i32 = b1
    let mut choice: i32 = 0
    if sec.counts[b2] < sec.counts[b1] {
        pick = b2
        choice = 1
    }
    let slot2: i32 = find_free_bit(sec.bitmap[pick], S_BUCKET)
    if slot2 < 0 {
        return -1
    }
    sec.bitmap[pick] = sec.bitmap[pick] | (1 << slot2)
    sec.counts[pick] = sec.counts[pick] + 1
    let pos2: i32 = P_SLOTS + pick * S_BUCKET + slot2
    values[pos2] = value
    pos_out[0] = pos2
    return TP_TABLE_BIT | (choice << 3) | slot2   # table bit + choice + slot
}

# Dereference a tiny pointer: recompute the bucket from the key and the
# pointer's choice bits. Returns the absolute slot, or -1 on a bad pointer.
function tp_dereference(pr: PrimaryTable, sec: SecondaryTable, key: i32, tp: i32) -> i32 {
    if (tp & TP_TABLE_BIT) == 0 {
        let b: i32 = hash_u32(key, 1) % P_BUCKETS
        let slot: i32 = tp & (P_BUCKET - 1)
        return b * P_BUCKET + slot
    }
    let choice: i32 = (tp >> 3) & 1
    let slot: i32 = tp & (S_BUCKET - 1)
    let mut b: i32 = hash_u32(key, 2) % S_BUCKETS
    if choice == 1 {
        b = hash_u32(key, 3) % S_BUCKETS
    }
    return P_SLOTS + b * S_BUCKET + slot
}

# Free a slot, clearing the bucket bitmap. The key is needed to find the
# bucket — exactly as in the paper's Free(k, p).
function tp_free(pr: PrimaryTable, sec: SecondaryTable, key: i32, tp: i32) {
    if (tp & TP_TABLE_BIT) == 0 {
        let b: i32 = hash_u32(key, 1) % P_BUCKETS
        let slot: i32 = tp & (P_BUCKET - 1)
        pr.bitmap[b] = pr.bitmap[b] & (0 - (1 << slot) - 1)
        pr.counts[b] = pr.counts[b] - 1
        return
    }
    let choice: i32 = (tp >> 3) & 1
    let slot: i32 = tp & (S_BUCKET - 1)
    let mut b: i32 = hash_u32(key, 2) % S_BUCKETS
    if choice == 1 {
        b = hash_u32(key, 3) % S_BUCKETS
    }
    sec.bitmap[b] = sec.bitmap[b] & (0 - (1 << slot) - 1)
    sec.counts[b] = sec.counts[b] - 1
}

# ============================================================================
# Registry of users (the paper's Alice/Bob/... model): each key owns at most
# one tiny pointer. Tracks expected position + value for verification.
# ============================================================================

struct Registry {
    key: ptr<i32>,     # REG_SIZE
    val: ptr<i32>,     # expected value per key
    tp: ptr<i32>,      # tiny pointer per key (-1 if not live)
    pos: ptr<i32>,     # expected absolute slot per key
    live: ptr<i32>,    # 0/1
    live_list: ptr<i32>,  # registry indices of live keys, compact
    next_idx: ptr<i32>,   # next free registry index (monotonic)
    live_top: ptr<i32>    # number of live keys
}

function registry_create() -> Registry {
    let rk: ptr<i32> = malloc(REG_SIZE * 4)
    let rv: ptr<i32> = malloc(REG_SIZE * 4)
    let rt: ptr<i32> = malloc(REG_SIZE * 4)
    let rp: ptr<i32> = malloc(REG_SIZE * 4)
    let rl: ptr<i32> = malloc(REG_SIZE * 4)
    let ll: ptr<i32> = malloc(REG_SIZE * 4)
    let nx: ptr<i32> = malloc(4)
    let lt: ptr<i32> = malloc(4)
    nx[0] = 0
    lt[0] = 0
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < REG_SIZE {
        rk[i] = 0
        rv[i] = 0
        rt[i] = -1
        rp[i] = -1
        rl[i] = 0
        ll[i] = -1
        i = i + 1
    }
    return Registry {
        key: rk, val: rv, tp: rt, pos: rp, live: rl,
        live_list: ll, next_idx: nx, live_top: lt
    }
}

function registry_destroy(reg: Registry) {
    free(reg.key)
    free(reg.val)
    free(reg.tp)
    free(reg.pos)
    free(reg.live)
    free(reg.live_list)
    free(reg.next_idx)
    free(reg.live_top)
}

# Insert a new key with a value into the dereference table.
function reg_insert(reg: ptr<Registry>, pr: PrimaryTable, sec: SecondaryTable,
                    values: ptr<i32>, key: i32, value: i32) -> i32 {
    let idx: i32 = reg[0].next_idx[0]
    if idx >= REG_SIZE {
        return 0
    }
    reg[0].next_idx[0] = idx + 1
    let mut pos: i32 = 0
    let tp: i32 = tp_allocate(pr, sec, values, key, value, &pos)
    if tp < 0 {
        return 0
    }
    reg[0].key[idx] = key
    reg[0].val[idx] = value
    reg[0].tp[idx] = tp
    reg[0].pos[idx] = pos
    reg[0].live[idx] = 1
    let top: i32 = reg[0].live_top[0]
    reg[0].live_list[top] = idx
    reg[0].live_top[0] = top + 1
    return 1
}

# Delete a uniformly random live key (used by the churn phase).
function reg_delete_random(reg: ptr<Registry>, pr: PrimaryTable, sec: SecondaryTable,
                           state: ptr<i32>) -> i32 {
    let top: i32 = reg[0].live_top[0]
    if top == 0 {
        return 0
    }
    let j: i32 = rng_next(state) % top
    let idx: i32 = reg[0].live_list[j]
    let key: i32 = reg[0].key[idx]
    let tp: i32 = reg[0].tp[idx]
    tp_free(pr, sec, key, tp)
    reg[0].live[idx] = 0
    reg[0].tp[idx] = -1
    reg[0].live_list[j] = reg[0].live_list[top - 1]
    reg[0].live_top[0] = top - 1
    return 1
}

# Verify EVERY live key: Dereference(key, tp) must return the recorded
# position, and the value stored there must match. Returns error count.
function reg_verify(reg: ptr<Registry>, pr: PrimaryTable, sec: SecondaryTable,
                    values: ptr<i32>) -> i32 {
    let mut errors: i32 = 0
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < REG_SIZE {
        if reg[0].live[i] == 1 {
            let pos: i32 = tp_dereference(pr, sec, reg[0].key[i], reg[0].tp[i])
            if pos != reg[0].pos[i] {
                errors = errors + 1
            }
            if pos == reg[0].pos[i] and values[pos] != reg[0].val[i] {
                errors = errors + 1
            }
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return errors
}

# ============================================================================
# Stable dictionary (Theorem 8): an open-addressing map from key → tiny
# pointer. Values live in the dereference table and never move; even a full
# map rebuild (rehash) cannot invalidate a tiny pointer.
# ============================================================================

struct DMap {
    key: ptr<i32>,    # DMAP_CAP
    tp: ptr<i32>,     # DMAP_CAP
    state: ptr<i32>,  # DMAP_CAP: 0 empty, 1 occupied
    seed: ptr<i32>    # current hash seed (changes on each rebuild)
}

function dmap_create() -> DMap {
    let dk: ptr<i32> = malloc(DMAP_CAP * 4)
    let dt: ptr<i32> = malloc(DMAP_CAP * 4)
    let ds: ptr<i32> = malloc(DMAP_CAP * 4)
    let sd: ptr<i32> = malloc(4)
    sd[0] = 7
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < DMAP_CAP {
        dk[i] = 0
        dt[i] = -1
        ds[i] = 0
        i = i + 1
    }
    return DMap { key: dk, tp: dt, state: ds, seed: sd }
}

function dmap_destroy(m: DMap) {
    free(m.key)
    free(m.tp)
    free(m.state)
    free(m.seed)
}

function dmap_find(m: ptr<DMap>, key: i32) -> i32 {
    let seed: i32 = m[0].seed[0]
    let mut idx: i32 = hash_u32(key, seed) % DMAP_CAP
    let mut probes: i32 = 0
    while probes < DMAP_CAP {
        if m[0].state[idx] == 0 {
            return 0 - idx - 1      # empty slot, encoded negative
        }
        if m[0].state[idx] == 1 and m[0].key[idx] == key {
            return idx + 1          # found, encoded positive
        }
        idx = (idx + 1) % DMAP_CAP
        probes = probes + 1
    }
    return 0
}

function dmap_insert(m: ptr<DMap>, key: i32, tp: i32) -> i32 {
    let slot: i32 = dmap_find(m, key)
    if slot == 0 {
        return 0
    }
    if slot > 0 {
        m[0].tp[slot - 1] = tp      # update in place
        return 1
    }
    let idx: i32 = 0 - slot - 1
    m[0].key[idx] = key
    m[0].tp[idx] = tp
    m[0].state[idx] = 1
    return 1
}

function dmap_lookup(m: ptr<DMap>, key: i32) -> i32 {
    let slot: i32 = dmap_find(m, key)
    if slot > 0 {
        return m[0].tp[slot - 1]
    }
    return -1
}

# NOTE: no dmap_delete — open addressing needs tombstones for safe deletion,
# and the demo only ever inserts + rebuilds. The paper's stable-dictionary
# transformation only requires Insert/Lookup/rebuild to preserve tiny pointers.

# Rebuild the map from scratch (simulates any dictionary-internal movement:
# resize, rehash, compaction). Allocates brand-new arrays with a different
# hash seed and swaps them in — tiny pointers are copied verbatim, so this
# proves they survive a full reallocation.
function dmap_rebuild(m: ptr<DMap>) -> i32 {
    let mut moved: i32 = 0
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < DMAP_CAP {
        if m[0].state[i] == 1 {
            moved = moved + 1
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    let nk_raw: ptr<u8> = malloc(DMAP_CAP * 4)
    let nt_raw: ptr<u8> = malloc(DMAP_CAP * 4)
    let ns_raw: ptr<u8> = malloc(DMAP_CAP * 4)
    let nk: ptr<i32> = nk_raw
    let nt: ptr<i32> = nt_raw
    let ns: ptr<i32> = ns_raw
    i = 0
    while i < DMAP_CAP {
        nk[i] = 0
        nt[i] = -1
        ns[i] = 0
        i = i + 1
    }
    let new_seed: i32 = m[0].seed[0] + 4
    i = 0
    while i < DMAP_CAP {
        if m[0].state[i] == 1 {
            let k: i32 = m[0].key[i]
            let t: i32 = m[0].tp[i]
            let mut idx: i32 = hash_u32(k, new_seed) % DMAP_CAP
            let mut probes: i32 = 0
            while probes < DMAP_CAP and ns[idx] == 1 {
                idx = (idx + 1) % DMAP_CAP
                probes = probes + 1
            }
            nk[idx] = k
            nt[idx] = t
            ns[idx] = 1
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    let old_k_raw: ptr<u8> = m[0].key
    let old_t_raw: ptr<u8> = m[0].tp
    let old_s_raw: ptr<u8> = m[0].state
    m[0].key = nk
    m[0].tp = nt
    m[0].state = ns
    m[0].seed[0] = new_seed
    free(old_k_raw)
    free(old_t_raw)
    free(old_s_raw)
    return moved
}

# ============================================================================
# Theorem 2 — VARIABLE-SIZE tiny pointers (paper Section 4, Proposition 1)
#
# The fixed-size construction caps every pointer at O(log log log n + log δ⁻¹)
# bits. Variable-size pointers break the log log log n barrier entirely:
# Theorem 2 gives pointers of O(1 + log δ⁻¹) EXPECTED bits, with a doubly-
# exponential concentration bound — Pr[size ≥ ℓ] ≤ 2^(−2^Ω(ℓ)).
#
# Construction (each of the n/log n CONTAINERS is an independent mini-table):
#   • hash key → one of n/log n containers; each holds at most s = c·log n keys;
#   • inside a container, log₂ s LEVELS; level i is a load-balancing table with
#     sᵢ = s/2ⁱ buckets of b slots each (b = 2 here);
#   • Lᵢ = #values stored in levels ≥ i (tables + overflow arrays); the
#     deterministic invariant Lᵢ ≤ sᵢ holds at all times;
#   • Allocate: descend i = 0, 1, … — try level-i table; on bucket-full, recurse
#     to level i+1 only if Lᵢ₊₁ < sᵢ₊₁, else place in level-i OVERFLOW ARRAY
#     (sᵢ slots; can never fill because Lᵢ ≤ sᵢ).
#
# Pointer encoding (self-delimiting, matches the paper's size accounting):
#   • table slot at level i:  Elias-γ(i+1)  +  1 table-bit  +  ⌈log₂b⌉ slot-bits
#                             = O(log i) bits;
#   • overflow slot at level i: Elias-γ(log₂s − i) + 1 + ⌈log₂ sᵢ⌉ = O(log sᵢ).
#   (Elias-γ(x) = 2⌊log₂x⌋+1 bits — a real prefix code, so these ARE the bit
#   lengths a concrete implementation would store.)
#
# Tail bound from the paper: Pr[level ≥ i] ≤ 1/bⁱ, and Pr[overflow at i] ≤
# 2^(−Ω(sᵢ)); since size(level i) = O(log i), Pr[size ≥ ℓ] ≤ 2^(−2^Ω(ℓ)).
# We measure exactly this: the empirical size distribution of every live
# pointer, checked against the theoretical curve.
#
# Concrete instance: n = 65536, log n = 16, containers = 4096, s = 32, b = 2,
# levels = 5, sᵢ = 32, 16, 8, 4, 2. Each container is 186 slots:
#   tables:   L0: 0–63   L1: 64–95   L2: 96–111  L3: 112–119  L4: 120–123
#   overflow: O0: 124–155 O1: 156–171 O2: 172–179 O3: 180–183 O4: 184–185
#
# Phase 5 (natural): 49152 uniform keys → ~98% get 3-bit pointers, ~2% get
# 5-bit, ~nothing deeper — the tail is invisible at this scale.
# Phase 6 (adversarial): 20 keys that hash to the SAME container AND the same
# level-0 bucket (found by offline bit-exact search) force a cascade down the
# levels; overflow arrays engage when Lᵢ₊₁ reaches sᵢ₊₁. Even under this
# worst case every pointer still dereferences correctly and sizes reach only
# 10–11 bits — versus 16 bits for a raw pointer.

const V_N_CONTAINERS: i32 = 4096      # n / log n
const V_S: i32 = 32                   # container capacity s = 2·log n
const V_B: i32 = 2                    # bucket slots
const V_LEVELS: i32 = 5               # log₂ s
const V_SLOTS_PER_CTR: i32 = 186      # tables 124 + overflow 62
const V_MAX_LIVE: i32 = 49152         # keys loaded; matches phase 1's count. Note:
#   Proposition 1 only promises Ω(1) load factor — here 49152/(4096·186) ≈ 6.5%.
#   Theorem 2's 1 − δ load factor comes from the paper's primary/secondary
#   reduction (this construction is the δn/2-slot secondary), not from this
#   standalone mini-table.

const R_MAX_LIVE: i32 = 49152       # relaxed-retrieval demo capacity (keys)
const RH_CAP: i32 = 131072          # H capacity — load ≤ 0.375 at 49152 keys
const CHURN_OPS: i32 = 30000        # Phase 2/3 dynamic churn op counts
const R_CHURN_OPS: i32 = 20000      # Phase 8 relaxed-retrieval churn op counts

# --- Phase 8b: Theorem 6 tradeoff — r levels of indirection (paper §6.2) ---
const RR_N: i32 = 16384            # keys per r-run (2^14; universe 2n = 2^15)
const RR_CHURN: i32 = 8000          # timed insert/delete ops per r-run
const RR_ADV: i32 = 64              # adversarial keys forced into one bucket chain
#   (fixed across bench sizes: the r=4 top bucket holds ≥ 2+4+16+42 ≥ 64 at
#   every log2n the bench runs, so the whole cascade always fits)

const BST_N: i32 = 16384            # BST nodes: keys 0..BST_N-1 inserted in order
const BST_STORM: i32 = 20000        # random rotations after the AVL build
const BST_NULL: i32 = -2            # null edge — must differ from the table's
#   free marker -1, so a null child never looks like a free table slot

const VD_CLASSES: i32 = 7        # value size classes j = 0..6 → slots of 1,2,4,8,16,32,64 bits
const VD_BUCKETS: i32 = 4096     # load-balancing buckets per class (power-of-two-choices)
const VD_VB: i32 = 8             # slots per bucket
const VD_MAX_KEYS: i32 = 49152   # keys in the variable-value dictionary

const KD_MAX_KEYS: i32 = 49152   # pairs in the variable-key + variable-value dict
const KD_CONTROL: i32 = 8192     # uniform 64+64-bit control pairs (fixed, not scaled)
const KDK_BIG: i32 = 65          # keys of ≥ 65 bits go through the key chain
const KDK_PTR_CLASS: i32 = 3     # base class holding the key's p₁ (8-bit slots)
const KDK_CONTROL_BIG: i32 = 1024  # uniform 256+64-bit control (fixed, not scaled)

# --- Phase 14: deamortized dynamic resizing (paper Section 6.1) ------------
const RV_CLASSES: i32 = 7        # same size classes as the VDTable
const RV_VB: i32 = 8             # slots per bucket
const RV_B0: i32 = 512           # initial buckets per class (grows ×2, shrinks ÷2)
const RV_B_MIN: i32 = 512        # shrink floor — never halve below this
const RV_BUDGET: i32 = 16        # values migrated per op (deamortized budget)
const RV_SCAN: i32 = 64          # registry entries examined per op
const RV_LOAD: i32 = 30000       # keys in the initial load (≈ 3 resize grows)
const RV_CHURN: i32 = 20000      # 55/45 insert/delete churn ops
const RV_DRAIN: i32 = 26000      # delete-heavy drain (3 shrinks)
const RV_RELOAD: i32 = 25000     # reload after drain (grow-after-shrink)

# --- Phase 15: r-levels of indirection (paper Theorem 9 proof, Section 6.5) --
const RL_KEYS: i32 = 12288       # keys in the r-levels dictionary
const RL_CHURN: i32 = 4000       # churn ops after the load
const RL_ARENA_B: i32 = 2048     # level-1 arena buckets per class — shared by
#   Phase 15's VALUE arena and Phase 12's KEY arena. Sized to the BIG-KEY
#   population of Phase 12 (≈10% of 49152 pairs = ≈4900 chained keys over 2
#   classes ≈ 1.7/bucket at 2048), NOT to either key space: the arena is the
#   paper's case-(b) auxiliary table 'small enough to take negligible space'
const RL_BIG: i32 = 65           # values of ≥ 65 bits go through the chain
const RL_PTR_CLASS: i32 = 3      # base class holding p₁ (8-bit slots)

# --- Phase 15 (r = 3 extension): the O(log log k) middle pointer p₂ ---------
const RL3_KEYS: i32 = 12288      # keys in the r=3 dictionary
const RL3_CHURN: i32 = 3000      # churn ops (big values move through the chain)
const RL3_ARENA_B: i32 = 512     # level-1 arena buckets per class (8-slot)
const RL3_P2_B: i32 = 512        # level-2 buckets per class (16-slot)
const RL3_HUGE: i32 = 4096       # values > 4096 bits are class-1 ('huge')
const RL3_PTR_CLASS: i32 = 3     # base class holding p₂ (8-bit slots)
const RL3_POOL_WORDS: i32 = 524288  # shared value word pool (4 MB)
const RL3_MAX_WORDS: i32 = 4096     # scratch buffer: biggest stored value (2^18 bits)

# --- Phase 16: optimal internal-memory stash (paper Theorem 10, Section 6.6) --
const ST_M: i32 = 8192        # keys in the stash (external load = m)
const ST_NB: i32 = 4096       # external buckets (two-choice placement)
const ST_B: i32 = 8           # slots per bucket → 4m external slots, load 1/4
const ST_EXT: i32 = 32768     # ST_NB × ST_B = 4m slots; the proof's dereference
#   table has load factor 1 − ε = 1/4 (ε = 3/4) — the intro's (1+ε)m is the
#   loose framing; the tiny pointer Θ(log ε⁻¹) is what the demo measures
const ST_BINS: i32 = 2048     # stash quotient bins (avg m/ST_BINS = 4 per bin)
const ST_Q_BITS: i32 = 11     # log2(ST_BINS) — quotient width; rest = h >> Q_BITS
const ST_C1_SALT: i32 = 8     # first candidate bucket (implicit, never stored)
const ST_C2_SALT: i32 = 9     # second candidate bucket (implicit, never stored)
const ST_Q_SALT: i32 = 7      # quotient + fingerprint rest (implicit, never stored)
const ST_V_SALT: i32 = 10     # value draw
const ST_CHURN: i32 = 6000    # churn ops after the load
const ST_QUERIES: i32 = 16384 # query-storm size (external-access accounting)

# table offset of level i: 128 - 128>>i  (0, 64, 96, 112, 120)
function v_tbl_off(i: i32) -> i32 {
    return 128 - (128 >> i)
}

# overflow offset of level i: 188 - 64>>i  (124, 156, 172, 180, 184)
function v_of_off(i: i32) -> i32 {
    return 188 - (64 >> i)
}

struct VarTable {
    slots: ptr<i32>,   # V_CONTAINERS × V_SLOTS_PER_CTR  (-1 = free)
    L: ptr<i32>        # V_CONTAINERS × V_LEVELS  (Lᵢ counters)
}

function vtable_create() -> VarTable {
    let raw_s: ptr<u8> = malloc(V_N_CONTAINERS * V_SLOTS_PER_CTR * 4)
    let raw_l: ptr<u8> = malloc(V_N_CONTAINERS * V_LEVELS * 4)
    let slots: ptr<i32> = raw_s
    let L: ptr<i32> = raw_l
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < V_N_CONTAINERS * V_SLOTS_PER_CTR {
        slots[i] = -1
        i = i + 1
    }
    i = 0
    while i < V_N_CONTAINERS * V_LEVELS {
        L[i] = 0
        i = i + 1
    }
    return VarTable { slots: slots, L: L }
}

function vtable_destroy(vt: VarTable) {
    let raw_s: ptr<u8> = vt.slots
    let raw_l: ptr<u8> = vt.L
    free(raw_s)
    free(raw_l)
}

# Pack a variable-size pointer: 1 flag bit + 3 level bits + 5 slot bits.
# flag 0 = load-balancing table, flag 1 = overflow array.
function vtp_pack(flag: i32, level: i32, slot: i32) -> i32 {
    return (flag << 8) | (level << 5) | slot
}

# Allocate `key` → `value`. Returns the variable-size pointer (>= 0), or -1 if
# the container is at capacity s (allocation fails, exactly as the paper's
# Allocate step 2). Writes the absolute slot to pos_out.
function vtp_allocate(vt: ptr<VarTable>, key: i32, value: i32, pos_out: ptr<i32>) -> i32 {
    let c: i32 = hash_u32(key, 4) % V_N_CONTAINERS
    if vt[0].L[c * V_LEVELS] >= V_S {
        return -1
    }
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < V_LEVELS {
        vt[0].L[c * V_LEVELS + i] = vt[0].L[c * V_LEVELS + i] + 1
        let s_i: i32 = V_S >> i
        let b: i32 = hash_u32(key, 5 + i) % s_i
        let base: i32 = c * V_SLOTS_PER_CTR + v_tbl_off(i) + b * V_B
        let mut j: i32 = 0
        while j < V_B {
            if vt[0].slots[base + j] == -1 {
                vt[0].slots[base + j] = value
                pos_out[0] = base + j
                return vtp_pack(0, i, j)
            }
            j = j + 1
        }
        # bucket full: next level only if L_{i+1} < s_{i+1}, else overflow array i
        let next: i32 = i + 1
        if next < V_LEVELS and vt[0].L[c * V_LEVELS + next] < (V_S >> next) {
            i = i + 1
        } else {
            let o: i32 = c * V_SLOTS_PER_CTR + v_of_off(i)
            let mut k: i32 = 0
            while k < s_i {
                if vt[0].slots[o + k] == -1 {
                    vt[0].slots[o + k] = value
                    pos_out[0] = o + k
                    return vtp_pack(1, i, k)
                }
                k = k + 1
            }
            return -1      # unreachable: Lᵢ ≤ sᵢ guarantees a free overflow slot
        }
    }
    return -1              # unreachable: last level's table always fits (L₄ ≤ 2 ≤ 2b)
}

# Dereference a variable-size pointer: recompute container, level, bucket.
function vtp_dereference(vt: ptr<VarTable>, key: i32, tp: i32) -> i32 {
    let flag: i32 = (tp >> 8) & 1
    let level: i32 = (tp >> 5) & 7
    let slot: i32 = tp & 31
    let c: i32 = hash_u32(key, 4) % V_N_CONTAINERS
    if flag == 0 {
        let b: i32 = hash_u32(key, 5 + level) % (V_S >> level)
        return c * V_SLOTS_PER_CTR + v_tbl_off(level) + b * V_B + slot
    }
    return c * V_SLOTS_PER_CTR + v_of_off(level) + slot
}

# Free: clear the slot and decrement L₀..L_level (the value was counted there).
function vtp_free(vt: ptr<VarTable>, key: i32, tp: i32) {
    let level: i32 = (tp >> 5) & 7
    let c: i32 = hash_u32(key, 4) % V_N_CONTAINERS
    let pos: i32 = vtp_dereference(vt, key, tp)
    vt[0].slots[pos] = -1
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i <= level {
        vt[0].L[c * V_LEVELS + i] = vt[0].L[c * V_LEVELS + i] - 1
        i = i + 1
    }
}

# Verify the deterministic invariants: Lᵢ ≤ sᵢ, bucket occupancy ≤ b,
# overflow occupancy ≤ sᵢ, container occupancy ≤ s. Returns error count.
function vtable_check(vt: ptr<VarTable>) -> i32 {
    let mut errors: i32 = 0
    let mut c: i32 = 0
    while c < V_N_CONTAINERS {
        let mut i: i32 = 0
        while i < V_LEVELS {
            if vt[0].L[c * V_LEVELS + i] > (V_S >> i) {
                errors = errors + 1
            }
            i = i + 1
        }
        # bucket occupancies
        i = 0
        while i < V_LEVELS {
            let s_i: i32 = V_S >> i
            let mut b: i32 = 0
            while b < s_i {
                let mut occ: i32 = 0
                let mut j: i32 = 0
                while j < V_B {
                    if vt[0].slots[c * V_SLOTS_PER_CTR + v_tbl_off(i) + b * V_B + j] != -1 {
                        occ = occ + 1
                    }
                    j = j + 1
                }
                if occ > V_B {
                    errors = errors + 1
                }
                b = b + 1
            }
            i = i + 1
        }
        # overflow occupancies
        i = 0
        while i < V_LEVELS {
            let s_i: i32 = V_S >> i
            let mut occ: i32 = 0
            let mut k: i32 = 0
            while k < s_i {
                if vt[0].slots[c * V_SLOTS_PER_CTR + v_of_off(i) + k] != -1 {
                    occ = occ + 1
                }
                k = k + 1
            }
            if occ > s_i {
                errors = errors + 1
            }
            i = i + 1
        }
        c = c + 1
    }
    return errors
}

# ---- registry operations for the variable-size table (reuses Registry) ----

function vreg_insert(reg: ptr<Registry>, vt: ptr<VarTable>, key: i32, value: i32) -> i32 {
    let idx: i32 = reg[0].next_idx[0]
    if idx >= REG_SIZE {
        return 0
    }
    let mut pos: i32 = 0
    let tp: i32 = vtp_allocate(vt, key, value, &pos)
    if tp < 0 {
        return 0                     # allocation failed: next_idx NOT consumed
    }
    reg[0].next_idx[0] = idx + 1
    reg[0].key[idx] = key
    reg[0].val[idx] = value
    reg[0].tp[idx] = tp
    reg[0].pos[idx] = pos
    reg[0].live[idx] = 1
    let top: i32 = reg[0].live_top[0]
    reg[0].live_list[top] = idx
    reg[0].live_top[0] = top + 1
    return 1
}

function vreg_delete_random(reg: ptr<Registry>, vt: ptr<VarTable>, state: ptr<i32>) -> i32 {
    let top: i32 = reg[0].live_top[0]
    if top == 0 {
        return 0
    }
    let j: i32 = rng_next(state) % top
    let idx: i32 = reg[0].live_list[j]
    let key: i32 = reg[0].key[idx]
    let tp: i32 = reg[0].tp[idx]
    vtp_free(vt, key, tp)
    reg[0].live[idx] = 0
    reg[0].tp[idx] = -1
    reg[0].live_list[j] = reg[0].live_list[top - 1]
    reg[0].live_top[0] = top - 1
    return 1
}

function vreg_verify(reg: ptr<Registry>, vt: ptr<VarTable>) -> i32 {
    let mut errors: i32 = 0
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < REG_SIZE {
        if reg[0].live[i] == 1 {
            let pos: i32 = vtp_dereference(vt, reg[0].key[i], reg[0].tp[i])
            if pos != reg[0].pos[i] {
                errors = errors + 1
            }
            if pos == reg[0].pos[i] and vt[0].slots[pos] != reg[0].val[i] {
                errors = errors + 1
            }
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return errors
}

# Evict every live key that hashes to container `cid` (used to clear the
# adversarial container before the cascade). Returns #evicted.
function vreg_evict_container(reg: ptr<Registry>, vt: ptr<VarTable>, cid: i32) -> i32 {
    let mut evicted: i32 = 0
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < reg[0].live_top[0] {
        let idx: i32 = reg[0].live_list[i]
        let c: i32 = hash_u32(reg[0].key[idx], 4) % V_N_CONTAINERS
        if c == cid {
            let key: i32 = reg[0].key[idx]
            let tp: i32 = reg[0].tp[idx]
            vtp_free(vt, key, tp)
            reg[0].live[idx] = 0
            reg[0].tp[idx] = -1
            reg[0].live_list[i] = reg[0].live_list[reg[0].live_top[0] - 1]
            reg[0].live_top[0] = reg[0].live_top[0] - 1
            evicted = evicted + 1
        } else {
            i = i + 1
        }
    }
    return evicted
}

# Elias-γ length of x (x ≥ 1): 2⌊log₂x⌋ + 1 bits.
function elias_bits(x: i32) -> i32 {
    let mut lg: i32 = 0
    let mut v: i32 = x
    while v > 1 {
        v = v >> 1
        lg = lg + 1
    }
    return 2 * lg + 1
}

# floor(log₂ x) for x ≥ 1 — used for the AVL height bound at any n.
function log2i(x: i32) -> i32 {
    let mut lg: i32 = 0
    let mut v: i32 = x >> 1
    while v > 0 {
        v = v >> 1
        lg = lg + 1
    }
    return lg
}

# Bit-length of a variable-size pointer (the paper's size accounting).
function vtp_size(tp: i32) -> i32 {
    let flag: i32 = (tp >> 8) & 1
    let level: i32 = (tp >> 5) & 7
    if flag == 0 {
        return elias_bits(level + 1) + 1 + 1          # level + table-bit + slot (⌈log₂b⌉=1)
    }
    return elias_bits(V_LEVELS - level) + 1 + (V_LEVELS - level)   # ℓ' + bit + ⌈log₂sᵢ⌉
}

# Empirical tail Pr[size ≥ ℓ] over all live keys, and the theory bound
# Σ_{i: size(i) ≥ ℓ} (1/2ⁱ + 2^(−sᵢ/4)). Returns 1 if empirical ≤ bound at
# every threshold (doubly-exponential tail confirmed), else 0.
function vtail_report(reg: ptr<Registry>) -> i32 {
    # count live pointers by size
    let mut hist: array<i32, 16> = [0]
    let mut total: i32 = 0
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < REG_SIZE {
        if reg[0].live[i] == 1 {
            let sz: i32 = vtp_size(reg[0].tp[i])
            hist[sz] = hist[sz] + 1
            total = total + 1
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    println("")
    println("  ── doubly-exponential tail bound ──")
    print("  measured: Pr[size ≥ ℓ] over ")
    print(total)
    println(" live pointers; theory: Pr[P ≥ ℓ] ≤ 2^(−2^Ω(ℓ))")
    println("")
    let thresholds: array<i32, 4> = [5, 7, 9, 11]
    let mut ok: i32 = 1
    i = 0
    while i < 4 {
        let ell: i32 = thresholds[i]
        let mut cnt: i32 = 0
        let mut s: i32 = ell
        while s < 16 {
            cnt = cnt + hist[s]
            s = s + 1
        }
        # theory bound: table levels with size ≥ ℓ → 1/2ⁱ; overflow → 2^(−sᵢ/4)
        let mut bound_tbl: f32 = 0.0
        let mut level: i32 = 0
        while level < V_LEVELS {
            let mut sz: i32 = elias_bits(level + 1) + 1 + 1
            if sz >= ell {
                let mut term: f32 = 1.0
                let mut k: i32 = 0
                while k < level {
                    term = term / 2.0
                    k = k + 1
                }
                bound_tbl = bound_tbl + term
            }
            sz = elias_bits(V_LEVELS - level) + 1 + (V_LEVELS - level)
            if sz >= ell {
                let mut of_term: f32 = 1.0
                let mut m: i32 = 0
                while m < (V_S >> level) / 4 {
                    of_term = of_term / 2.0
                    m = m + 1
                }
                bound_tbl = bound_tbl + of_term
            }
            level = level + 1
        }
        let emp: f32 = (cnt as f32) / (total as f32)
        let mut pass: i32 = 0
        if emp <= bound_tbl {
            pass = 1
        }
        if pass == 0 {
            ok = 0
        }
        print("  ℓ=")
        print(ell)
        print("   empirical ")
        print(emp)
        print("   bound ")
        print(bound_tbl)
        if pass == 1 {
            println("  ✓")
        } else {
            println("  ✗")
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    println("")
    return ok
}

# ============================================================================
# Theorem 6 — RELAXED RETRIEVAL (paper Section 6.2, Theorem 6)
#
# Classic dynamic retrieval (Insert/Delete/Query on k-bit keys with v-bit
# values) provably costs Ω(log log n) metadata bits per value — even for
# v = 1. Relax the contract: Insert(x, y) returns an O(1)-expected hint r;
# Query(x, r) and Delete(x, r) present both. The hint is just a variable-size
# tiny pointer, and the pair (x, r) does NOT have to encode the position of y:
# a small auxiliary structure helps recover it.
#
# Construction: reuse the Theorem-2 dereference table but treat its store as
# unused — 'we need not even allocate space for it'. Allocate(x) → hint r;
# s_x = Dereference(x, r) is a slot UNIQUE to x. The value y is stored in a
# hash table H keyed by slot: Query(x,r) = H[s_x], Delete(x,r) = H[s_x] + Free.
# H's keys come from the tiny universe [2n], so log C(2n,n) = O(n) bits by
# Stirling's approximation — H costs nv + O(n) total. H is pure auxiliary
# metadata: it may rehash, move, or be rebuilt; hints never care.
#
# Phase 7 loads 49152 keys and measures the hint-size distribution against the
# Ω(log log n) = 4-bit lower bound at n = 2^16. Phase 8 churns (tombstoned
# deletes — deletion is part of the API), forces 20 adversarial hints through
# one container, then REBUILDS H with a new seed: every hint must survive.
# ============================================================================

struct RHash {
    key: ptr<i32>,    # RH_CAP — slot numbers (-1 empty)
    val: ptr<i32>,    # RH_CAP — v-bit values
    state: ptr<i32>,  # 0 empty, 1 occupied, 2 tombstone
    seed: ptr<i32>
}

function rhash_create() -> RHash {
    let k_raw: ptr<u8> = malloc(RH_CAP * 4)
    let v_raw: ptr<u8> = malloc(RH_CAP * 4)
    let s_raw: ptr<u8> = malloc(RH_CAP * 4)
    let k: ptr<i32> = k_raw
    let v: ptr<i32> = v_raw
    let st: ptr<i32> = s_raw
    let sd: ptr<i32> = malloc(4)
    sd[0] = 13
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < RH_CAP {
        k[i] = -1
        v[i] = -1
        st[i] = 0
        i = i + 1
    }
    return RHash { key: k, val: v, state: st, seed: sd }
}

function rhash_destroy(h: RHash) {
    let k_raw: ptr<u8> = h.key
    let v_raw: ptr<u8> = h.val
    let s_raw: ptr<u8> = h.state
    free(k_raw)
    free(v_raw)
    free(s_raw)
    free(h.seed)
}

# Find `slot` in H. Returns: 0 ≤ idx < RH_CAP if found; RH_CAP if the table
# is full; otherwise 0 - idx - 1 where idx is the insertion point (first
# tombstone, else first empty slot, in the probe chain). Lookups must NOT stop
# at tombstones — only at empty slots.
function rhash_find(h: ptr<RHash>, slot: i32) -> i32 {
    let seed: i32 = h[0].seed[0]
    let mut idx: i32 = hash_u32(slot, seed) % RH_CAP
    let mut first_gap: i32 = -1
    let mut probes: i32 = 0
    while probes < RH_CAP {
        let st: i32 = h[0].state[idx]
        if st == 0 {
            let mut ip: i32 = idx
            if first_gap >= 0 {
                ip = first_gap
            }
            return 0 - ip - 1
        }
        if st == 1 and h[0].key[idx] == slot {
            return idx
        }
        if st == 2 and first_gap < 0 {
            first_gap = idx
        }
        idx = (idx + 1) % RH_CAP
        probes = probes + 1
    }
    # Wrapped the whole table with no empty slot: reuse a tombstone if we saw
    # one (reclaimable), else the table is genuinely full.
    if first_gap >= 0 {
        return 0 - first_gap - 1
    }
    return RH_CAP
}

function rhash_insert(h: ptr<RHash>, slot: i32, val: i32) -> i32 {
    let r: i32 = rhash_find(h, slot)
    if r >= 0 and r < RH_CAP {
        h[0].val[r] = val
        return 1
    }
    if r == RH_CAP {
        return 0
    }
    let idx: i32 = 0 - r - 1
    h[0].key[idx] = slot
    h[0].val[idx] = val
    h[0].state[idx] = 1
    return 1
}

function rhash_lookup(h: ptr<RHash>, slot: i32) -> i32 {
    let seed: i32 = h[0].seed[0]
    let mut idx: i32 = hash_u32(slot, seed) % RH_CAP
    let mut probes: i32 = 0
    while probes < RH_CAP {
        let st: i32 = h[0].state[idx]
        if st == 0 {
            return -1
        }
        if st == 1 and h[0].key[idx] == slot {
            return h[0].val[idx]
        }
        idx = (idx + 1) % RH_CAP
        probes = probes + 1
    }
    return -1
}

function rhash_delete(h: ptr<RHash>, slot: i32) {
    let seed: i32 = h[0].seed[0]
    let mut idx: i32 = hash_u32(slot, seed) % RH_CAP
    let mut probes: i32 = 0
    while probes < RH_CAP {
        let st: i32 = h[0].state[idx]
        if st == 0 {
            return
        }
        if st == 1 and h[0].key[idx] == slot {
            h[0].state[idx] = 2
            return
        }
        idx = (idx + 1) % RH_CAP
        probes = probes + 1
    }
}

# Rebuild H from scratch with a fresh seed, compacting away tombstones.
# This is the 'auxiliary metadata moves' demonstration: the dictionary is free
# to rehash/reposition while every hint stays valid.
function rhash_rebuild(h: ptr<RHash>) -> i32 {
    let mut n: i32 = 0
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < RH_CAP {
        if h[0].state[i] == 1 {
            n = n + 1
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    let nk_raw: ptr<u8> = malloc(RH_CAP * 4)
    let nv_raw: ptr<u8> = malloc(RH_CAP * 4)
    let ns_raw: ptr<u8> = malloc(RH_CAP * 4)
    let nk: ptr<i32> = nk_raw
    let nv: ptr<i32> = nv_raw
    let ns: ptr<i32> = ns_raw
    i = 0
    while i < RH_CAP {
        nk[i] = -1
        nv[i] = -1
        ns[i] = 0
        i = i + 1
    }
    let new_seed: i32 = h[0].seed[0] + 4
    i = 0
    while i < RH_CAP {
        if h[0].state[i] == 1 {
            let k: i32 = h[0].key[i]
            let v: i32 = h[0].val[i]
            let mut idx: i32 = hash_u32(k, new_seed) % RH_CAP
            let mut probes: i32 = 0
            while probes < RH_CAP and ns[idx] == 1 {
                idx = (idx + 1) % RH_CAP
                probes = probes + 1
            }
            nk[idx] = k
            nv[idx] = v
            ns[idx] = 1
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    let old_k_raw: ptr<u8> = h[0].key
    let old_v_raw: ptr<u8> = h[0].val
    let old_s_raw: ptr<u8> = h[0].state
    h[0].key = nk
    h[0].val = nv
    h[0].state = ns
    h[0].seed[0] = new_seed
    free(old_k_raw)
    free(old_v_raw)
    free(old_s_raw)
    return n
}

function rhash_count(h: ptr<RHash>) -> i32 {
    let mut n: i32 = 0
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < RH_CAP {
        if h[0].state[i] == 1 {
            n = n + 1
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return n
}

# ---- registry operations for relaxed retrieval (reuses Registry) ----------

# Insert(x, y): allocate a slot, hand the tiny pointer to the USER as the
# hint r, and store (s_x, y) in H. The dereference table's store is never
# read — we only use the uniqueness of the slot it hands out.
function rreg_insert(reg: ptr<Registry>, vt: ptr<VarTable>, h: ptr<RHash>,
                     key: i32, value: i32) -> i32 {
    let idx: i32 = reg[0].next_idx[0]
    if idx >= REG_SIZE {
        return 0
    }
    let mut pos: i32 = 0
    # Paper precondition: every Insert is on a key x not already in S. The
    # registry never deduplicates, so duplicate keys would simply get their
    # own slot + H cell (each entry verifies independently — still correct).
    let tp: i32 = vtp_allocate(vt, key, 1, &pos)   # store unused — marker 1
    if tp < 0 {
        return 0
    }
    let oki: i32 = rhash_insert(h, pos, value)
    if oki == 0 {
        vtp_free(vt, key, tp)
        return 0
    }
    reg[0].next_idx[0] = idx + 1
    reg[0].key[idx] = key
    reg[0].val[idx] = value
    reg[0].tp[idx] = tp            # the hint r (a variable-size tiny pointer)
    reg[0].pos[idx] = pos          # the unique slot s_x
    reg[0].live[idx] = 1
    let top: i32 = reg[0].live_top[0]
    reg[0].live_list[top] = idx
    reg[0].live_top[0] = top + 1
    return 1
}

# Delete(x, r): remove s_x from H (tombstone) and Free the table slot.
function rreg_delete_random(reg: ptr<Registry>, vt: ptr<VarTable>, h: ptr<RHash>,
                            state: ptr<i32>) -> i32 {
    let top: i32 = reg[0].live_top[0]
    if top == 0 {
        return 0
    }
    let j: i32 = rng_next(state) % top
    let idx: i32 = reg[0].live_list[j]
    let key: i32 = reg[0].key[idx]
    let tp: i32 = reg[0].tp[idx]
    rhash_delete(h, reg[0].pos[idx])
    vtp_free(vt, key, tp)
    reg[0].live[idx] = 0
    reg[0].tp[idx] = -1
    reg[0].live_list[j] = reg[0].live_list[top - 1]
    reg[0].live_top[0] = top - 1
    return 1
}

# Evict every live key that hashes to container `cid`, deleting its slot
# from BOTH the dereference table (vtp_free) and H (rhash_delete) — the
# auxiliary structure must never hold an entry whose key is gone.
function rreg_evict_container(reg: ptr<Registry>, vt: ptr<VarTable>, h: ptr<RHash>,
                              cid: i32) -> i32 {
    let mut evicted: i32 = 0
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < reg[0].live_top[0] {
        let idx: i32 = reg[0].live_list[i]
        let c: i32 = hash_u32(reg[0].key[idx], 4) % V_N_CONTAINERS
        if c == cid {
            let key: i32 = reg[0].key[idx]
            let tp: i32 = reg[0].tp[idx]
            rhash_delete(h, reg[0].pos[idx])
            vtp_free(vt, key, tp)
            reg[0].live[idx] = 0
            reg[0].tp[idx] = -1
            reg[0].live_list[i] = reg[0].live_list[reg[0].live_top[0] - 1]
            reg[0].live_top[0] = reg[0].live_top[0] - 1
            evicted = evicted + 1
        } else {
            i = i + 1
        }
    }
    return evicted
}

# Verify EVERY live key: Query(x, r) = H[Dereference(x, r)] must return the
# recorded value. dups_out counts lookup failures caused by two keys sharing
# a slot (uniqueness is the whole construction — it must be zero).
function rreg_verify(reg: ptr<Registry>, vt: ptr<VarTable>, h: ptr<RHash>,
                     dups_out: ptr<i32>) -> i32 {
    let mut errors: i32 = 0
    dups_out[0] = 0
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < REG_SIZE {
        if reg[0].live[i] == 1 {
            let pos: i32 = vtp_dereference(vt, reg[0].key[i], reg[0].tp[i])
            if pos != reg[0].pos[i] {
                errors = errors + 1
            }
            let got: i32 = rhash_lookup(h, reg[0].pos[i])
            if got != reg[0].val[i] {
                errors = errors + 1
                dups_out[0] = dups_out[0] + 1
            }
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return errors
}

# ============================================================================
# Phase 8b — Theorem 6 TRADEOFF: r levels of indirection (paper Section 6.2)
#
# Theorem 6 promises the tradeoff curve: with r levels of indirection,
# insertions/deletions take O(r) time and the total space is nv + O(n log⁽ʳ⁾ n)
# bits. This sub-phase sweeps r = 1..4 on a relaxed-retrieval store whose
# dereference table is an r-level chain of keyed buckets, and measures BOTH
# sides of the curve:
#
#   · TIME — Insert(x) descends level 0 → 1 → … until a bucket has room;
#     Delete(x, r) descends to the retriever's level. Worst case (every level
#     a single bucket, adversarial placement) the descent walks all r levels:
#     O(r) per insertion/deletion. Expected case stays O(1) — overflows are
#     rare at load 1/2 — and that is measured too.
#   · SPACE — the level-0 bucket capacity is 2^⌈log⁽ʳ⁾ n⌉, so the retriever
#     (the base slot code the user holds) costs w₀ = ⌈log⁽ʳ⁾ n⌉ bits — the
#     "O(n log⁽ʳ⁾ n)" term. Measured at n = 2^14: w₀ = 15 → 4 → 2 → 1 bits/key
#     for r = 1..4 (r = 1 is the paper's (1 + log n)-bit slot number over the
#     [2n] universe — the spendthrift end), while the values cost nv
#     untouched. Deeper levels hold exponentially larger buckets
#     (2^w₀, 2^2w₀, …, up to the full 2^(1+log n) value universe), so the
#     chain telescopes: Σᵢ wᵢ = 1 + log n exactly.
#
# Geometry (universe 2n = 2^(1+log n)): level i has NBᵢ = 2^(1+log n − wᵢ)
# buckets of Bᵢ = 2^wᵢ slots, wᵢ = 2^i·w₀ (i < r−1) and w_{r−1} = the
# remainder. Natural run: n keys over 2n slots/level (load 1/2). Adversarial
# run: NBᵢ = 1 at every level, so all RR_ADV keys collide at every level and
# cascade to the deepest bucket — the measured O(r) worst case.
#
# Values live in the level-(r−1) buckets (the nv bits); intermediate entries
# are keyed with full-key compare and EXACT-slot retrievers (no tombstones —
# the retriever names the precise slot, so delete is a single clear).

struct RrChain {
    key: ptr<i32>,    # total slots, -1 free
    val: ptr<i32>,    # v-bit values
    off: ptr<i32>,    # per-level base offsets (4)
    nb: ptr<i32>,     # per-level bucket counts (4)
    bw: ptr<i32>,     # per-level bucket widths w_i (4): B_i = 2^w_i
    r: i32,
    fp: i32           # salt base — bucket = hash(key, fp + level) % nb[level]
}

# ceil(log2 x) for x ≥ 1.
function rr_clog2(x: i32) -> i32 {
    let mut lg: i32 = 0
    let mut v: i32 = x - 1
    while v > 0 {
        v = v >> 1
        lg = lg + 1
    }
    return lg
}

# w₀ = ⌈log⁽ʳ⁾(2n)⌉: the r-th iterated ceil-logarithm of (1 + log n).
# r = 1 → 1 + log n (the raw slot width); r = 2 → ⌈log₂(1 + log n)⌉; …
function rr_w0(r: i32, log2n: i32) -> i32 {
    let mut w: i32 = log2n
    let mut k: i32 = 0
    while k < r - 1 {
        w = rr_clog2(w)
        k = k + 1
    }
    if w < 1 {
        w = 1
    }
    return w
}

function rr_chain_create(r: i32, log2n: i32, single_bucket: i32) -> RrChain {
    let w0: i32 = rr_w0(r, log2n)
    let wr: ptr<i32> = malloc(4 * 4)
    let nr: ptr<i32> = malloc(4 * 4)
    let orr: ptr<i32> = malloc(4 * 4)
    let mut off_acc: i32 = 0
    let mut sum: i32 = 0
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < r {
        let mut wi: i32 = 0
        if i < r - 1 {
            wi = w0 << i                # w0, 2w0, 4w0, … (exponentially larger)
        } else {
            wi = log2n - sum            # remainder — chain telescopes to 1 + log n
            if wi < 1 {
                wi = 1
            }
        }
        wr[i] = wi
        let mut nb: i32 = 1
        if single_bucket == 0 {
            nb = 1 << (log2n - wi)
        }
        nr[i] = nb
        orr[i] = off_acc
        off_acc = off_acc + (nb << wi)
        sum = sum + wi
        i = i + 1
    }
    let total: i32 = off_acc
    let k_raw: ptr<u8> = malloc(total * 4)
    let v_raw: ptr<u8> = malloc(total * 4)
    let kk: ptr<i32> = k_raw
    let vv: ptr<i32> = v_raw
    i = 0
    while i < total {
        kk[i] = -1
        vv[i] = 0
        i = i + 1
    }
    return RrChain { key: kk, val: vv, off: orr, nb: nr, bw: wr, r: r, fp: 19 }
}

function rr_chain_destroy(c: RrChain) {
    let k_raw: ptr<u8> = c.key
    let v_raw: ptr<u8> = c.val
    free(k_raw)
    free(v_raw)
    free(c.off)
    free(c.nb)
    free(c.bw)
}

# First slot index of `key`'s bucket at `level` (bucket id is hash-derived,
# exactly like the container id in the Theorem-2 table — never stored).
function rr_bucket_base(c: ptr<RrChain>, level: i32, key: i32) -> i32 {
    let b: i32 = hash_u32(key, c[0].fp + level) % c[0].nb[level]
    return c[0].off[level] + (b << c[0].bw[level])
}

# Insert (x, y): descend levels until a bucket has room. The retriever is the
# (level, slot) pair — level in bits 16-17, slot in bits 0-15. Returns the
# descent depth (level landed, 0-based), or -1 if every bucket is full.
function rr_insert(c: ptr<RrChain>, reg: ptr<Registry>, key: i32, value: i32) -> i32 {
    let mut level: i32 = 0
    while level < c[0].r {
        let base: i32 = rr_bucket_base(c, level, key)
        let bsz: i32 = 1 << c[0].bw[level]
        # probe cyclically from a hash-derived start — expected 2 probes at
        # load 1/2 regardless of bucket size (the paper's O(1)-expected case).
        let start: i32 = hash_u32(key, c[0].fp + 40 + level) % bsz
        let mut k: i32 = 0
        while k < bsz {
            let s: i32 = (start + k) % bsz
            let idx: i32 = base + s
            if c[0].key[idx] == key {
                # re-insert: refresh the value in place (the registry entry is
                # untouched — the demo only ever inserts FRESH distinct keys,
                # so this path is just a safety net for generality)
                c[0].val[idx] = value
                return level
            }
            if c[0].key[idx] == -1 {
                c[0].key[idx] = key
                c[0].val[idx] = value
                let ridx: i32 = reg[0].next_idx[0]
                if ridx >= REG_SIZE {
                    return -2
                }
                reg[0].next_idx[0] = ridx + 1
                reg[0].key[ridx] = key
                reg[0].val[ridx] = value
                reg[0].tp[ridx] = (level << 16) | s
                reg[0].pos[ridx] = idx
                reg[0].live[ridx] = 1
                let top: i32 = reg[0].live_top[0]
                reg[0].live_list[top] = ridx
                reg[0].live_top[0] = top + 1
                return level
            }
            k = k + 1
        }
        level = level + 1
    }
    return -1
}

# Query/Dereference(x, r): the bucket is recomputed from the key, the retriever
# names the exact slot inside it — one probe, O(1) expected.
function rr_deref(c: ptr<RrChain>, key: i32, retriever: i32) -> i32 {
    let level: i32 = (retriever >> 16) & 3
    let s: i32 = retriever & 0xFFFF
    let idx: i32 = rr_bucket_base(c, level, key) + s
    if c[0].key[idx] == key {
        return c[0].val[idx]
    }
    return -1
}

# Delete(x, r): clear the exact slot — no tombstone needed.
function rr_delete(c: ptr<RrChain>, key: i32, retriever: i32) {
    let level: i32 = (retriever >> 16) & 3
    let s: i32 = retriever & 0xFFFF
    let idx: i32 = rr_bucket_base(c, level, key) + s
    if c[0].key[idx] == key {
        c[0].key[idx] = -1
    }
}

# Verify every live key: Query(x, r) must return the recorded value. dups_out
# counts lookups that returned a different value (a slot corruption).
function rr_verify(c: ptr<RrChain>, reg: ptr<Registry>, dups_out: ptr<i32>) -> i32 {
    let mut errors: i32 = 0
    dups_out[0] = 0
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < REG_SIZE {
        if reg[0].live[i] == 1 {
            let got: i32 = rr_deref(c, reg[0].key[i], reg[0].tp[i])
            if got != reg[0].val[i] {
                errors = errors + 1
                dups_out[0] = dups_out[0] + 1
            }
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return errors
}

# Bit-length of a retriever: the slot width w_level (the log⁽ʳ⁾ n term) plus
# the ⌈log₂ r⌉ level tag (0 for r = 1, where the level is implicit).
function rr_bits(c: ptr<RrChain>, retriever: i32) -> i32 {
    let level: i32 = (retriever >> 16) & 3
    let mut tag: i32 = 0
    if c[0].r > 1 {
        tag = rr_clog2(c[0].r)
    }
    return c[0].bw[level] + tag
}

# ============================================================================
# Theorem 7 — SUCCINCT ROTATION-BASED BINARY SEARCH TREES (paper Section 6.3)
#
# There are at most 4ⁿ ordered binary trees on n nodes, so a tree's pointer
# structure fits in O(n) bits. Classic succinct-tree encodings support leaf
# insert/delete, but NOT rotations — and AVL / red-black / splay trees live
# on rotations. Theorem 7 removes that gap: make the child pointers tiny.
#
# Construction (black-box over Theorem 6): each node stores key/value plus two
# tiny RETRIEVERS — r₁ for the left child keyed on x◦0, r₂ for the right child
# keyed on x◦1. The relaxed-retrieval store becomes the node array: the slot
# Dereference(x◦c, r_c) holds the child's index. Navigation = one query = O(1).
# Adding/removing an edge (x, z) = one store overwrite = O(r) (constant here).
# A ROTATION rewrites only the 2-3 touched edges: no node moves, and — the key
# point — no retriever ever changes. The tree structure therefore stays
# na + nb + O(n) bits (distinct keys give na = Ω(n log n), so the tree is
# succinct), while rotations remain constant-time.
#
# Phase 9 builds an AVL tree from 16384 keys inserted IN ORDER — the worst
# case that forces maximal rotation — with children reached only through
# tiny retrievers; every retriever, slot and node position is verified, and
# the pointer size (≈6 bits/node vs 32 bits raw) is measured.
# Phase 10 storms the tree with 20000 random rotations: rotations preserve
# in-order order by definition, and no node or retriever may move.
# ============================================================================

struct BSTree {
    key: ptr<i32>,   # BST_N — key of node i (also its fixed array position)
    val: ptr<i32>,   # BST_N — value of node i
    lh: ptr<i32>,    # BST_N — left-child TINY RETRIEVER (key x◦0)
    rh: ptr<i32>,    # BST_N — right-child TINY RETRIEVER (key x◦1)
    lc: ptr<i32>,    # BST_N — recorded left child index (BST_NULL = none)
    rc: ptr<i32>,    # BST_N — recorded right child index
    ls: ptr<i32>,    # BST_N — recorded left-edge SLOT (retriever integrity)
    rs: ptr<i32>,    # BST_N — recorded right-edge SLOT
    hgt: ptr<i32>,   # BST_N — AVL height of the subtree rooted at node i
    n: ptr<i32>,     # node count — positions are append-only, never move
    rot: ptr<i32>    # rotation counter (both phases)
}

function bstree_create() -> BSTree {
    let k_raw: ptr<u8> = malloc(BST_N * 4)
    let v_raw: ptr<u8> = malloc(BST_N * 4)
    let lh_raw: ptr<u8> = malloc(BST_N * 4)
    let rh_raw: ptr<u8> = malloc(BST_N * 4)
    let lc_raw: ptr<u8> = malloc(BST_N * 4)
    let rc_raw: ptr<u8> = malloc(BST_N * 4)
    let ls_raw: ptr<u8> = malloc(BST_N * 4)
    let rs_raw: ptr<u8> = malloc(BST_N * 4)
    let hg_raw: ptr<u8> = malloc(BST_N * 4)
    let n_raw: ptr<u8> = malloc(4)
    let rt_raw: ptr<u8> = malloc(4)
    let k: ptr<i32> = k_raw
    let v: ptr<i32> = v_raw
    let lh: ptr<i32> = lh_raw
    let rh: ptr<i32> = rh_raw
    let lc: ptr<i32> = lc_raw
    let rc: ptr<i32> = rc_raw
    let ls: ptr<i32> = ls_raw
    let rs: ptr<i32> = rs_raw
    let hg: ptr<i32> = hg_raw
    let nn: ptr<i32> = n_raw
    let rt: ptr<i32> = rt_raw
    nn[0] = 0
    rt[0] = 0
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < BST_N {
        k[i] = -1
        v[i] = -1
        lh[i] = -1
        rh[i] = -1
        lc[i] = BST_NULL
        rc[i] = BST_NULL
        ls[i] = -1
        rs[i] = -1
        hg[i] = 0
        i = i + 1
    }
    return BSTree {
        key: k, val: v, lh: lh, rh: rh, lc: lc, rc: rc,
        ls: ls, rs: rs, hgt: hg, n: nn, rot: rt
    }
}

function bstree_destroy(t: BSTree) {
    free(t.key)
    free(t.val)
    free(t.lh)
    free(t.rh)
    free(t.lc)
    free(t.rc)
    free(t.ls)
    free(t.rs)
    free(t.hgt)
    free(t.n)
    free(t.rot)
}

# Child index of node nidx on side (0=left, 1=right) — the ONLY way to
# navigate: dereference the tiny retriever to its slot, read the node array.
function bst_child(t: ptr<BSTree>, vt: ptr<VarTable>, nidx: i32, side: i32) -> i32 {
    let key2: i32 = (t[0].key[nidx] << 1) | side
    let mut tp: i32 = t[0].lh[nidx]
    if side == 1 {
        tp = t[0].rh[nidx]
    }
    let pos: i32 = vtp_dereference(vt, key2, tp)
    return vt[0].slots[pos]
}

# Set the edge (nidx, side) → child. ONE O(1) store overwrite; the retriever
# and the slot never change — this is what keeps the structure O(n) bits.
function bst_set_child(t: ptr<BSTree>, vt: ptr<VarTable>, nidx: i32, side: i32, child: i32) {
    let key2: i32 = (t[0].key[nidx] << 1) | side
    let mut tp: i32 = t[0].lh[nidx]
    if side == 1 {
        tp = t[0].rh[nidx]
    }
    let pos: i32 = vtp_dereference(vt, key2, tp)
    vt[0].slots[pos] = child
    if side == 0 {
        t[0].lc[nidx] = child
    } else {
        t[0].rc[nidx] = child
    }
}

function bst_height(t: ptr<BSTree>, nidx: i32) -> i32 {
    if nidx == BST_NULL {
        return 0
    }
    return t[0].hgt[nidx]
}

function bst_update_height(t: ptr<BSTree>, vt: ptr<VarTable>, nidx: i32) {
    let hl: i32 = bst_height(t, bst_child(t, vt, nidx, 0))
    let hr: i32 = bst_height(t, bst_child(t, vt, nidx, 1))
    let mut h: i32 = hl
    if hr > h {
        h = hr
    }
    t[0].hgt[nidx] = h + 1
}

# Left-rotate at x (x's right child z becomes the subtree root). Constant-time:
# 2 edge overwrites + height fixes; no node moves, no retriever changes.
function bst_rotate_left(t: ptr<BSTree>, vt: ptr<VarTable>, x: i32) -> i32 {
    let z: i32 = bst_child(t, vt, x, 1)
    if z == BST_NULL {
        return x
    }
    let a: i32 = bst_child(t, vt, z, 0)
    bst_set_child(t, vt, x, 1, a)
    bst_set_child(t, vt, z, 0, x)
    bst_update_height(t, vt, x)
    bst_update_height(t, vt, z)
    t[0].rot[0] = t[0].rot[0] + 1
    return z
}

function bst_rotate_right(t: ptr<BSTree>, vt: ptr<VarTable>, x: i32) -> i32 {
    let z: i32 = bst_child(t, vt, x, 0)
    if z == BST_NULL {
        return x
    }
    let a: i32 = bst_child(t, vt, z, 1)
    bst_set_child(t, vt, x, 0, a)
    bst_set_child(t, vt, z, 1, x)
    bst_update_height(t, vt, x)
    bst_update_height(t, vt, z)
    t[0].rot[0] = t[0].rot[0] + 1
    return z
}

# AVL rebalance at nidx; returns the (possibly rotated) subtree root.
function bst_balance(t: ptr<BSTree>, vt: ptr<VarTable>, nidx: i32) -> i32 {
    bst_update_height(t, vt, nidx)
    let hl: i32 = bst_height(t, bst_child(t, vt, nidx, 0))
    let hr: i32 = bst_height(t, bst_child(t, vt, nidx, 1))
    if hl - hr > 1 {
        # left-heavy: LR case — rotate the left child left first
        let lc_: i32 = bst_child(t, vt, nidx, 0)
        let lc_hl: i32 = bst_height(t, bst_child(t, vt, lc_, 0))
        let lc_hr: i32 = bst_height(t, bst_child(t, vt, lc_, 1))
        if lc_hr > lc_hl {
            let nr: i32 = bst_rotate_left(t, vt, lc_)
            bst_set_child(t, vt, nidx, 0, nr)
            bst_update_height(t, vt, nidx)
        }
        return bst_rotate_right(t, vt, nidx)
    }
    if hr - hl > 1 {
        # right-heavy: RL case
        let rc_: i32 = bst_child(t, vt, nidx, 1)
        let rc_hl: i32 = bst_height(t, bst_child(t, vt, rc_, 0))
        let rc_hr: i32 = bst_height(t, bst_child(t, vt, rc_, 1))
        if rc_hl > rc_hr {
            let nr: i32 = bst_rotate_right(t, vt, rc_)
            bst_set_child(t, vt, nidx, 1, nr)
            bst_update_height(t, vt, nidx)
        }
        return bst_rotate_left(t, vt, nidx)
    }
    return nidx
}

# Create a new node: append at position n (positions NEVER move) and allocate
# two tiny retrievers — one per edge — with the store seeded to BST_NULL.
function bst_node_new(t: ptr<BSTree>, vt: ptr<VarTable>, key: i32, value: i32) -> i32 {
    let idx: i32 = t[0].n[0]
    if idx >= BST_N {
        return -1
    }
    let mut p0: i32 = 0
    let mut p1: i32 = 0
    let lh: i32 = vtp_allocate(vt, (key << 1) | 0, BST_NULL, &p0)
    if lh < 0 {
        return -1
    }
    let rh: i32 = vtp_allocate(vt, (key << 1) | 1, BST_NULL, &p1)
    if rh < 0 {
        vtp_free(vt, (key << 1) | 0, lh)
        return -1
    }
    t[0].key[idx] = key
    t[0].val[idx] = value
    t[0].lh[idx] = lh
    t[0].rh[idx] = rh
    t[0].lc[idx] = BST_NULL
    t[0].rc[idx] = BST_NULL
    t[0].ls[idx] = p0
    t[0].rs[idx] = p1
    t[0].hgt[idx] = 1
    t[0].n[0] = idx + 1
    return idx
}

# Recursive AVL insert. Returns the new subtree root. Sorted insert order is
# the worst case for a plain BST — AVL rotations keep the height O(log n).
function bst_insert_avl(t: ptr<BSTree>, vt: ptr<VarTable>, root: i32,
                        key: i32, value: i32) -> i32 {
    if root == BST_NULL {
        return bst_node_new(t, vt, key, value)
    }
    if key < t[0].key[root] {
        let nl: i32 = bst_insert_avl(t, vt, bst_child(t, vt, root, 0), key, value)
        bst_set_child(t, vt, root, 0, nl)
        return bst_balance(t, vt, root)
    }
    let nr: i32 = bst_insert_avl(t, vt, bst_child(t, vt, root, 1), key, value)
    bst_set_child(t, vt, root, 1, nr)
    return bst_balance(t, vt, root)
}

# Rotation storm helper: rotate the node holding `key` left (side=0) or right
# (side=1); re-link the parent edge on the way back up. Returns new subtree
# root. Rotating a node with no child on that side is a no-op.
# Precondition (paper's model): every key is present, so the recursion never
# bottoms out at BST_NULL while searching — an absent key would null an edge.
function bst_rotate_key(t: ptr<BSTree>, vt: ptr<VarTable>, root: i32,
                        key: i32, side: i32) -> i32 {
    if root == BST_NULL {
        return BST_NULL
    }
    if key == t[0].key[root] {
        if side == 0 {
            return bst_rotate_left(t, vt, root)
        }
        return bst_rotate_right(t, vt, root)
    }
    if key < t[0].key[root] {
        let nl: i32 = bst_rotate_key(t, vt, bst_child(t, vt, root, 0), key, side)
        bst_set_child(t, vt, root, 0, nl)
        return root
    }
    let nr: i32 = bst_rotate_key(t, vt, bst_child(t, vt, root, 1), key, side)
    bst_set_child(t, vt, root, 1, nr)
    return root
}

# In-order traversal THROUGH tiny-pointer edges. err_out counts out-of-order
# pairs; count_out counts visited nodes.
function bst_inorder_check(t: ptr<BSTree>, vt: ptr<VarTable>, nidx: i32,
                           last_out: ptr<i32>, count_out: ptr<i32>, err_out: ptr<i32>) {
    if nidx == BST_NULL {
        return
    }
    bst_inorder_check(t, vt, bst_child(t, vt, nidx, 0), last_out, count_out, err_out)
    if last_out[0] >= 0 and t[0].key[nidx] <= last_out[0] {
        err_out[0] = err_out[0] + 1
    }
    last_out[0] = t[0].key[nidx]
    count_out[0] = count_out[0] + 1
    bst_inorder_check(t, vt, bst_child(t, vt, nidx, 1), last_out, count_out, err_out)
}

# AVL balance check: returns the tree height, or -1 if any node is unbalanced.
function bst_balance_check(t: ptr<BSTree>, vt: ptr<VarTable>, nidx: i32) -> i32 {
    if nidx == BST_NULL {
        return 0
    }
    let hl: i32 = bst_balance_check(t, vt, bst_child(t, vt, nidx, 0))
    if hl < 0 {
        return -1
    }
    let hr: i32 = bst_balance_check(t, vt, bst_child(t, vt, nidx, 1))
    if hr < 0 {
        return -1
    }
    let mut d: i32 = hl - hr
    if d < 0 {
        d = 0 - d
    }
    if d > 1 {
        return -1
    }
    let mut h: i32 = hl
    if hr > h {
        h = hr
    }
    return h + 1
}

# Retriever integrity over the WHOLE node array (not just reachable nodes):
# every retriever must still dereference to its recorded slot, the store must
# hold the recorded child, and node positions must never have moved.
function bst_ptr_check(t: ptr<BSTree>, vt: ptr<VarTable>) -> i32 {
    let mut errors: i32 = 0
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < t[0].n[0] {
        let kl: i32 = (t[0].key[i] << 1) | 0
        let kr: i32 = (t[0].key[i] << 1) | 1
        let pl: i32 = vtp_dereference(vt, kl, t[0].lh[i])
        let pr: i32 = vtp_dereference(vt, kr, t[0].rh[i])
        if pl != t[0].ls[i] {
            errors = errors + 1
        }
        if pr != t[0].rs[i] {
            errors = errors + 1
        }
        if vt[0].slots[pl] != t[0].lc[i] {
            errors = errors + 1
        }
        if vt[0].slots[pr] != t[0].rc[i] {
            errors = errors + 1
        }
        if t[0].key[i] != i {
            errors = errors + 1
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return errors
}

# ============================================================================
# Output helpers
# ============================================================================

# ============================================================================
# Theorem 9 — VARIABLE-SIZE VALUE DICTIONARIES (paper Section 6.5)
#
# Black-box transform: any key-value dictionary designed to store uniform-size
# values can store ARBITRARY-LENGTH values (up to O(1) machine words) with
# constant time overhead and an additional log⁽ʳ⁾ n + O(log vᵢ) bits per
# vᵢ-bit value (paper Eq. 4: O(m log⁽ʳ⁾ m) + Σ(vᵢ + O(log vᵢ))).
#
# The mechanism: SIZE CLASSES. A value of v bits is placed in class
# j = ⌈log₂ v⌉; class j has its own dereference-table storage whose slots are
# exactly 2^j bits. The key hashes to a bucket inside the class (two choices,
# pick the lighter), and the tiny pointer packs (class, choice, slot) in
# ⌈log₂ classes⌉ + 1 + ⌈log₂ b⌉ = 7 bits — O(log v) for v ≤ 64. A uniform
# 64-bit dictionary would pay 64 bits for EVERY value; here a v-bit value pays
# 2^⌈log v⌉ (rounded payload) + 7 (pointer). Phase 11 itself fixes v ≤ 64 (2
# machine words) and measures the size-class space bound directly; the paper's
# r-levels-of-indirection chain (pointers sized O(log k), O(log log k), ...)
# and the deamortized dynamic resizing are implemented on top in Phases 14-15
# (deamortized resizing lifts the Θ(m) copy; the r-levels chain lifts the cap
# to 256 bits behind a second-level tiny pointer). Phase 11 loads 49152 keys
# with a skewed value-size distribution
# (70% ≤ 8 bits, 20% ≤ 16, 10% ≤ 64), verifies every value bit-exactly,
# churns, and adds a uniform 64-bit control that shows the bound tightening.
# ============================================================================

struct VDTable {
    bitmap: ptr<i32>,    # VD_CLASSES × VD_BUCKETS occupancy words
    counts: ptr<i32>,    # VD_CLASSES × VD_BUCKETS per-bucket occupancy
    store: ptr<i64>      # VD_CLASSES × VD_BUCKETS × VD_VB slots (i64 each)
}

function vdtable_create() -> VDTable {
    let raw_b: ptr<u8> = malloc(VD_CLASSES * VD_BUCKETS * 4)
    let raw_c: ptr<u8> = malloc(VD_CLASSES * VD_BUCKETS * 4)
    let raw_s: ptr<u8> = malloc(VD_CLASSES * VD_BUCKETS * VD_VB * 8)
    let bm: ptr<i32> = raw_b
    let ct: ptr<i32> = raw_c
    let st: ptr<i64> = raw_s
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < VD_CLASSES * VD_BUCKETS {
        bm[i] = 0
        ct[i] = 0
        i = i + 1
    }
    i = 0
    while i < VD_CLASSES * VD_BUCKETS * VD_VB {
        st[i] = 0
        i = i + 1
    }
    return VDTable { bitmap: bm, counts: ct, store: st }
}

function vdtable_destroy(vt: VDTable) {
    free(vt.bitmap)
    free(vt.counts)
    free(vt.store)
}

# Smallest class whose slot fits `bits` bits: ⌈log₂ bits⌉ (0 for bits ≤ 1).
function vd_class_of(bits: i32) -> i32 {
    let mut j: i32 = 0
    let mut cap: i32 = 1
    while cap < bits {
        cap = cap * 2
        j = j + 1
    }
    return j
}

# Class slot capacity in bits: 2^j.
function vd_cap(j: i32) -> i32 {
    return 1 << j
}

# Pack a variable-value pointer: 3 class bits + 1 choice bit + 3 slot bits.
function vd_pack(j: i32, choice: i32, slot: i32) -> i32 {
    return (j << 4) | (choice << 3) | slot
}

# Recompute the absolute slot from key + pointer (like every other dereference
# table: the key supplies the bucket, the pointer the position within it).
function vd_dereference(vt: ptr<VDTable>, key: i32, tp: i32) -> i32 {
    let j: i32 = (tp >> 4) & 7
    let choice: i32 = (tp >> 3) & 1
    let slot: i32 = tp & 7
    let mut b: i32 = hash_u32(key, 31 + j) % VD_BUCKETS
    if choice == 1 {
        b = hash_u32(key, 41 + j) % VD_BUCKETS
    }
    return (j * VD_BUCKETS + b) * VD_VB + slot
}

# Allocate `value` (of `bits` bits) for `key`. Power-of-two-choices within the
# class: pick the lighter of two buckets. Returns the 7-bit pointer, or -1 if
# both buckets are full (should not happen at these loads).
function vd_allocate(vt: ptr<VDTable>, key: i32, value: i64, bits: i32,
                     pos_out: ptr<i32>) -> i32 {
    let j: i32 = vd_class_of(bits)
    let b1: i32 = hash_u32(key, 31 + j) % VD_BUCKETS
    let b2: i32 = hash_u32(key, 41 + j) % VD_BUCKETS
    let mut pick: i32 = b1
    let mut choice: i32 = 0
    if vt[0].counts[j * VD_BUCKETS + b2] < vt[0].counts[j * VD_BUCKETS + b1] {
        pick = b2
        choice = 1
    }
    let slot: i32 = find_free_bit(vt[0].bitmap[j * VD_BUCKETS + pick], VD_VB)
    if slot < 0 {
        return -1
    }
    vt[0].bitmap[j * VD_BUCKETS + pick] = vt[0].bitmap[j * VD_BUCKETS + pick] | (1 << slot)
    vt[0].counts[j * VD_BUCKETS + pick] = vt[0].counts[j * VD_BUCKETS + pick] + 1
    let pos: i32 = (j * VD_BUCKETS + pick) * VD_VB + slot
    vt[0].store[pos] = value
    pos_out[0] = pos
    return vd_pack(j, choice, slot)
}

function vd_free(vt: ptr<VDTable>, key: i32, tp: i32) {
    let j: i32 = (tp >> 4) & 7
    let choice: i32 = (tp >> 3) & 1
    let slot: i32 = tp & 7
    let mut b: i32 = hash_u32(key, 31 + j) % VD_BUCKETS
    if choice == 1 {
        b = hash_u32(key, 41 + j) % VD_BUCKETS
    }
    vt[0].bitmap[j * VD_BUCKETS + b] = vt[0].bitmap[j * VD_BUCKETS + b] & (0 - (1 << slot) - 1)
    vt[0].counts[j * VD_BUCKETS + b] = vt[0].counts[j * VD_BUCKETS + b] - 1
}

# Per-class invariant check: no bucket may exceed b slots. Returns error count.
function vdtable_check(vt: ptr<VDTable>) -> i32 {
    let mut errors: i32 = 0
    let mut j: i32 = 0
    while j < VD_CLASSES {
        let mut b: i32 = 0
        while b < VD_BUCKETS {
            if vt[0].counts[j * VD_BUCKETS + b] > VD_VB {
                errors = errors + 1
            }
            b = b + 1
        }
        j = j + 1
    }
    return errors
}

# ---- registry operations for the variable-value dictionary ----------------
# Reuses Registry for keys/tp/pos/live; values and bit-lengths live in the
# parallel arrays vv (i64) and vb (i32) so 64-bit values round-trip exactly.

function vdreg_insert(reg: ptr<Registry>, vv: ptr<i64>, vb: ptr<i32>,
                      vt: ptr<VDTable>, key: i32, value: i64, bits: i32) -> i32 {
    let idx: i32 = reg[0].next_idx[0]
    if idx >= REG_SIZE {
        return 0
    }
    let mut pos: i32 = 0
    let tp: i32 = vd_allocate(vt, key, value, bits, &pos)
    if tp < 0 {
        return 0
    }
    reg[0].next_idx[0] = idx + 1
    reg[0].key[idx] = key
    vv[idx] = value
    vb[idx] = bits
    reg[0].tp[idx] = tp
    reg[0].pos[idx] = pos
    reg[0].live[idx] = 1
    let top: i32 = reg[0].live_top[0]
    reg[0].live_list[top] = idx
    reg[0].live_top[0] = top + 1
    return 1
}

function vdreg_delete_random(reg: ptr<Registry>, vt: ptr<VDTable>, state: ptr<i32>) -> i32 {
    let top: i32 = reg[0].live_top[0]
    if top == 0 {
        return 0
    }
    let j: i32 = rng_next(state) % top
    let idx: i32 = reg[0].live_list[j]
    let key: i32 = reg[0].key[idx]
    let tp: i32 = reg[0].tp[idx]
    vd_free(vt, key, tp)
    reg[0].live[idx] = 0
    reg[0].tp[idx] = -1
    reg[0].live_list[j] = reg[0].live_list[top - 1]
    reg[0].live_top[0] = top - 1
    return 1
}

# Verify EVERY live key: dereferenced slot = recorded, stored i64 = recorded
# value bit-exactly, and the pointer's class fits the recorded bit-length.
function vdreg_verify(reg: ptr<Registry>, vv: ptr<i64>, vb: ptr<i32>,
                      vt: ptr<VDTable>) -> i32 {
    let mut errors: i32 = 0
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < REG_SIZE {
        if reg[0].live[i] == 1 {
            let pos: i32 = vd_dereference(vt, reg[0].key[i], reg[0].tp[i])
            if pos != reg[0].pos[i] {
                errors = errors + 1
            }
            if pos == reg[0].pos[i] and vt[0].store[pos] != vv[i] {
                errors = errors + 1
            }
            if vd_cap((reg[0].tp[i] >> 4) & 7) < vb[i] {
                errors = errors + 1
            }
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return errors
}

# Skewed value-size draw + deterministic value for `key`: 70% ≤ 8 bits,
# 20% ≤ 16, 10% up to 64 ('arbitrary lengths up to O(1) machine words').
function vd_make_value(state: ptr<i32>, key: i32, bits_out: ptr<i32>) -> i64 {
    let r: i32 = rng_next(state) % 100
    let mut bits: i32 = 0
    if r < 70 {
        bits = 1 + (rng_next(state) % 8)
    } else {
        if r < 90 {
            bits = 9 + (rng_next(state) % 8)
        } else {
            bits = 17 + (rng_next(state) % 48)
        }
    }
    bits_out[0] = bits
    let h: i32 = hash_u32(key, 77)
    let mut mask: i64 = -1
    if bits < 64 {
        mask = ((1 as i64) << bits) - 1
    }
    return (h as i64) & mask
}

function banner5() {
    println("")
    println("╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗")
    println("║  Theorem 9 — VARIABLE-SIZE VALUE DICTIONARIES                        ║")
    println("║  Σ(vᵢ + O(log vᵢ)) bits for arbitrary-length values                  ║")
    println("╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝")
    println("")
    println("Any key-value dictionary designed for uniform-size values can be made")
    println("to support ARBITRARY-LENGTH values with constant time overhead and an")
    println("additional log⁽ʳ⁾ n + O(log vᵢ) bits per vᵢ-bit value (Theorem 9).")
    println("")
    println("Construction: values are grouped into SIZE CLASSES — a v-bit value")
    println("lives in class ⌈log₂ v⌉, whose slots are 2^⌈log v⌉ bits — and each")
    println("class owns its own dereference-table storage, keyed on the key like")
    println("every other tiny-pointer table. The key finds the class bucket; the")
    println("tiny pointer (class + choice + slot, O(log v) bits) names the slot.")
    println("")
}

# ============================================================================
# Theorem 9's keys analogue (Section 6.5 remark) — VARIABLE-SIZE KEYS
#
# The paper states Theorem 9 for "a key-value dictionary … designed to store
# fixed-length keys" and remarks that the same techniques work without
# modification. The size-class construction is symmetric: apply it to the KEY
# ARRAY and a k-bit key costs 2^⌈log k⌉ + O(log k) bits instead of a uniform
# 64-bit slot per key. Phase 12 stores BOTH sides of each (key, value) pair
# in size-classed tables and measures the COMBINED space.
#
# Implementation: reuse the VDTable geometry (7 classes × 4096 buckets × 8
# i64 slots, power-of-two-choices) for a KEY table kvt and a VALUE table vvt,
# keyed on the same logical key. The KEY side additionally runs the Phase-15
# r-levels chain: keys of 65-256 bits live in a compact key arena (RLTable)
# behind a 5-bit p₁, held in an 8-bit base class-3 slot with flag bit 7 set;
# keys ≤ 64 bits store directly in kvt. One registry entry per pair holds the
# key's pointer/position in reg.tp/reg.pos (the value's in parallel arrays);
# the key's four expected words live in kw0..kw3, its bit-length in kb, its
# p₁ and arena offset in kc1/kca, the value's payload + bits in vv/vb. Every-
# thing verifies bit-exactly through the chain.
#
# The key-chain registry ops are defined below the RLTable machinery (they
# call rl_allocate/rl_deref/rl_free), alongside the Phase 15 section.
# ============================================================================

function banner6() {
    println("")
    println("╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗")
    println("║  Theorem 9 (keys analogue) — VARIABLE-SIZE KEY DICTIONARIES          ║")
    println("║  Σ(kᵢ + O(log kᵢ)) bits for arbitrary-length keys                    ║")
    println("╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝")
    println("")
    println("Theorem 9 is stated for fixed-length keys, but its construction is")
    println("symmetric (the paper's remark: 'the same techniques work … without")
    println("modification'). A k-bit KEY lives in size class ⌈log₂ k⌉ with 2^⌈log k⌉-")
    println("bit slots, so the key array also costs Σ(2^⌈log kᵢ⌉ + O(log kᵢ)) instead")
    println("of a uniform 64-bit slot per key.")
    println("")
    println("Phase 12 stores BOTH sides of each pair size-classed — a KEY table and")
    println("a VALUE table keyed on the same logical key — and reports the combined")
    println("key+value space versus a uniform 64+64-bit baseline.")
    println("")
}

# ============================================================================
# Section 5 — THE LOWER BOUNDS (Theorems 3–5)
#
# Everything above is an UPPER bound (a construction). Section 5 proves these
# constructions are asymptotically optimal — no scheme can beat them:
#
#   Theorem 3 (variable-size pointers): expected size s = Ω(log δ⁻¹). At
#     δ = 1/4 that is log₂4 = 2 bits; the demo's variable pointers average
#     ≈ 3 bits.
#   Theorem 4 (fixed-size pointers): s = Ω(log log log n + log δ⁻¹). At
#     n = 2^16, δ = 1/4: log₂log₂log₂2^16 = 2, log₂4 = 2 → floor = 4 bits;
#     the demo's fixed pointers are 6 bits.
#   Theorem 5 (balls-and-bins, Vöcking): ANY sequential rule that gives each
#     of m balls d random bin choices leaves a fullest bin of Ω((log log m)/d).
#     This is the engine of Theorem 4: with s-bit pointers each key has only
#     d = 2^s choices, so the fullest bin cannot be pushed below
#     Ω((log log m)/2^s) — but a dereference table must keep every bin at O(1)
#     (each bin maps to n/m slots), forcing 2^s = Ω(log log n), i.e.
#     s = Ω(log log log n).
#
# Phase 13 demonstrates all three empirically:
#   A. Simulate the Theorem-5 process (least-loaded placement — the BEST
#      rule) and verify the fullest bin obeys F(s)·2^s ≥ log₂log₂m at every
#      pointer budget s — the Ω((log log m)/d) floor, measured;
#   B. Mask the live 6-bit pointers to s bits and count the FORCED
#      dereference collisions (unique slots stop being nameable): 0 at s = 6,
#      thousands below it — the scheme provably cannot spend fewer bits;
#   C. Re-measure the live variable-size pointer mean against the Theorem-3
#      floor log₂δ⁻¹.
# ============================================================================

const LB_S_MAX: i32 = 6          # pointer budgets s = 0..6 swept in Part A

# Place m balls into m bins, each ball choosing the least-loaded of d = 2^s
# bins (the BEST possible sequential rule — Theorem 5 lower-bounds even this).
# Returns the fullest bin. `bins` must be zeroed by the caller.
function lb_place(bins: ptr<i32>, m: i32, d: i32) -> i32 {
    let mut maxl: i32 = 0
    let mut x: i32 = 0
    while x < m {
        let mut best: i32 = 0
        let mut best_load: i32 = 2147483647
        let mut j: i32 = 0
        while j < d {
            let b: i32 = hash_u32(x, 20 + j) % m
            if bins[b] < best_load {
                best_load = bins[b]
                best = b
            }
            j = j + 1
        }
        bins[best] = bins[best] + 1
        if bins[best] > maxl {
            maxl = bins[best]
        }
        x = x + 1
    }
    return maxl
}

# Count keys whose pointer, truncated to s bits, dereferences to a slot
# already claimed by another key (a FORCED collision — uniqueness broken).
# At s = 6 (the real width) this is 0; below it, the scheme cannot name
# distinct slots.
function lb_mask_collisions(reg: ptr<Registry>, pr: PrimaryTable, sec: SecondaryTable,
                            s: i32) -> i32 {
    let mask: i32 = (1 << s) - 1
    let occ_raw: ptr<u8> = malloc(N_SLOTS * 4)
    let occ: ptr<i32> = occ_raw
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < N_SLOTS {
        occ[i] = 0
        i = i + 1
    }
    let mut coll: i32 = 0
    i = 0
    while i < REG_SIZE {
        if reg[0].live[i] == 1 {
            let mtp: i32 = reg[0].tp[i] & mask
            let pos: i32 = tp_dereference(pr, sec, reg[0].key[i], mtp)
            if occ[pos] == 1 {
                coll = coll + 1
            }
            occ[pos] = 1
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    free(occ_raw)
    return coll
}

function banner7() {
    println("")
    println("╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗")
    println("║  Section 5 — THE LOWER BOUNDS (Theorems 3-5)                         ║")
    println("║  no scheme can beat the floors the constructions achieve             ║")
    println("╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝")
    println("")
    println("Theorems 1-2 are upper bounds. Section 5 closes the loop: ANY tiny-")
    println("pointer scheme must spend at least:")
    println("  Theorem 3 (variable-size):  s = Ω(log δ⁻¹)   — 2 bits at δ = 1/4")
    println("  Theorem 4 (fixed-size):     s = Ω(log log log n + log δ⁻¹)")
    println("                              — 4 bits at n = 2^16, δ = 1/4")
    println("  Theorem 5 (balls-and-bins): fullest bin Ω((log log m)/2^s) under ANY")
    println("    rule — with s-bit pointers each key has only 2^s choices, and the")
    println("    paper proves even the best placement rule cannot dodge the floor.")
    println("")
    println("Phase 13 measures all three: the Theorem-5 floor directly (Part A),")
    println("the forced collisions a smaller pointer budget creates on the live")
    println("table (Part B), and the variable-size mean vs log₂δ⁻¹ (Part C).")
    println("")
}

# ============================================================================
# Phase 14 — DEAMORTIZED DYNAMIC RESIZING (paper Section 6.1, Theorem 4 proof)
# ============================================================================
# The Theorem-9 construction keeps each dereference table at a load factor of
# 1 - O(1/j'), which forces the tables to change size as the live count
# changes. Section 6.1 (zone-aggregated resizing) does this WITHOUT any
# Θ(m)-time operation: the table is rebuilt component-by-component and the
# rebuild is spread over the following operations. The Theorem-4 proof spells
# it out: "the time needed to rebuild a component of size s is Θ(s), so the
# dynamic resizing of Bi can be deamortized to take O(1) time per operation."
#
# Phase 14 implements exactly that for the variable-value dictionary's class
# storage: a two-generation RVTable whose per-class bucket arrays double
# (grow) or halve (shrink), with the migration from the old generation to the
# new one running in the background at a fixed per-operation budget:
#   · the op that crosses a load threshold starts a transition — O(1): it
#     allocates the new generation (the zeroing of its bitmap/count words is
#     itself part of the budget);
#   · every subsequent op zeroes RV_BUDGET words of the new generation and
#     examines RV_SCAN registry entries, migrating up to RV_BUDGET live
#     values (rewriting their tiny pointers to the new generation, exactly
#     the job of the paper's per-zone pointer lists);
#   · when the sweep completes, the old generation is freed and the epoch
#     flips — every live pointer now reads the new generation.
# The tiny pointer gains one generation bit (8 bits total) — the paper's
# "add Θ(log k) bits to each tiny pointer to indicate which dereference
# table is being pointed into".
#
# The demo runs the SAME workload (initial load, churn, drain, reload) twice:
#   (1) naive one-shot resizing — the triggering op copies every live value
#       at once, the textbook Θ(m) worst case;
#   (2) deamortized resizing — every op does O(1) work.
# It reports the worst-case per-op cost in both: work units (a deterministic
# count of words zeroed + values migrated) and wall-clock nanoseconds.
# ============================================================================

# Two-generation resizable value table. Storage for a physical generation
# exists iff its bk field is nonzero; the epoch field says which generation
# is canonical. A transition migrates live values from the epoch generation
# into the other one, then frees the old and flips the epoch.
struct RVTable {
    bm0: ptr<i32>, ct0: ptr<i32>, st0: ptr<i64>,
    bm1: ptr<i32>, ct1: ptr<i32>, st1: ptr<i64>,
    bk0: i32, bk1: i32,          # buckets per class in each generation
    epoch: i32,                  # canonical generation (0 or 1)
    phase: i32,                  # 0 = idle, 1 = growing, 2 = shrinking
    cursor: i32,                 # registry sweep cursor of the migration
    zero: i32                    # words of the new generation zeroed so far
}

function rvtable_create() -> RVTable {
    let raw_b0: ptr<u8> = malloc(RV_CLASSES * RV_B0 * 4)
    let raw_c0: ptr<u8> = malloc(RV_CLASSES * RV_B0 * 4)
    let raw_s0: ptr<u8> = malloc(RV_CLASSES * RV_B0 * RV_VB * 8)
    let raw_b1: ptr<u8> = malloc(RV_CLASSES * RV_B0 * 4)
    let raw_c1: ptr<u8> = malloc(RV_CLASSES * RV_B0 * 4)
    let raw_s1: ptr<u8> = malloc(RV_CLASSES * RV_B0 * RV_VB * 8)
    let b0: ptr<i32> = raw_b0
    let c0: ptr<i32> = raw_c0
    let s0: ptr<i64> = raw_s0
    let b1: ptr<i32> = raw_b1
    let c1: ptr<i32> = raw_c1
    let s1: ptr<i64> = raw_s1
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < RV_CLASSES * RV_B0 {
        b0[i] = 0
        c0[i] = 0
        b1[i] = 0
        c1[i] = 0
        i = i + 1
    }
    i = 0
    while i < RV_CLASSES * RV_B0 * RV_VB {
        s0[i] = 0
        s1[i] = 0
        i = i + 1
    }
    return RVTable {
        bm0: b0, ct0: c0, st0: s0,
        bm1: b1, ct1: c1, st1: s1,
        bk0: RV_B0, bk1: RV_B0,
        epoch: 0, phase: 0, cursor: 0, zero: 0
    }
}

function rvtable_destroy(rvt: ptr<RVTable>) {
    if rvt[0].bk0 > 0 {
        free(rvt[0].bm0)
        free(rvt[0].ct0)
        free(rvt[0].st0)
    }
    if rvt[0].bk1 > 0 {
        free(rvt[0].bm1)
        free(rvt[0].ct1)
        free(rvt[0].st1)
    }
}

# Buckets per class in physical generation pa (0 or 1).
function rv_buckets(rvt: ptr<RVTable>, pa: i32) -> i32 {
    if pa == 0 {
        return rvt[0].bk0
    }
    return rvt[0].bk1
}

function rv_bm(rvt: ptr<RVTable>, pa: i32) -> ptr<i32> {
    if pa == 0 {
        return rvt[0].bm0
    }
    return rvt[0].bm1
}

function rv_ct(rvt: ptr<RVTable>, pa: i32) -> ptr<i32> {
    if pa == 0 {
        return rvt[0].ct0
    }
    return rvt[0].ct1
}

function rv_st(rvt: ptr<RVTable>, pa: i32) -> ptr<i64> {
    if pa == 0 {
        return rvt[0].st0
    }
    return rvt[0].st1
}

# Generation bit of a pointer (bit 7). Pointers are 8 bits: gen + class 3 +
# choice 1 + slot 3 (the paper's Θ(log k) extra bits for k = 2 tables).
function rv_gen(tp: i32) -> i32 {
    return (tp >> 7) & 1
}

# Recompute the absolute slot from key + pointer, reading the generation the
# pointer names (epoch-independent — every live pointer names its own array).
function rv_deref(rvt: ptr<RVTable>, key: i32, tp: i32) -> i32 {
    let pa: i32 = rv_gen(tp)
    let j: i32 = (tp >> 4) & 7
    let choice: i32 = (tp >> 3) & 1
    let slot: i32 = tp & 7
    let B: i32 = rv_buckets(rvt, pa)
    let mut b: i32 = hash_u32(key, 31 + j) % B
    if choice == 1 {
        b = hash_u32(key, 41 + j) % B
    }
    return (j * B + b) * RV_VB + slot
}

# Allocate into physical generation pa. Mirror of vd_allocate with a variable
# bucket count and a generation bit on the pointer.
function rv_alloc(rvt: ptr<RVTable>, pa: i32, key: i32, value: i64, bits: i32,
                  pos_out: ptr<i32>) -> i32 {
    let j: i32 = vd_class_of(bits)
    let B: i32 = rv_buckets(rvt, pa)
    let bm: ptr<i32> = rv_bm(rvt, pa)
    let ct: ptr<i32> = rv_ct(rvt, pa)
    let st: ptr<i64> = rv_st(rvt, pa)
    let b1: i32 = hash_u32(key, 31 + j) % B
    let b2: i32 = hash_u32(key, 41 + j) % B
    let mut pick: i32 = b1
    let mut choice: i32 = 0
    if ct[j * B + b2] < ct[j * B + b1] {
        pick = b2
        choice = 1
    }
    let slot: i32 = find_free_bit(bm[j * B + pick], RV_VB)
    if slot < 0 {
        return -1
    }
    bm[j * B + pick] = bm[j * B + pick] | (1 << slot)
    ct[j * B + pick] = ct[j * B + pick] + 1
    let pos: i32 = (j * B + pick) * RV_VB + slot
    st[pos] = value
    pos_out[0] = pos
    return (pa << 7) | vd_pack(j, choice, slot)
}

function rv_free(rvt: ptr<RVTable>, key: i32, tp: i32) {
    let pa: i32 = rv_gen(tp)
    let j: i32 = (tp >> 4) & 7
    let choice: i32 = (tp >> 3) & 1
    let slot: i32 = tp & 7
    let B: i32 = rv_buckets(rvt, pa)
    let mut b: i32 = hash_u32(key, 31 + j) % B
    if choice == 1 {
        b = hash_u32(key, 41 + j) % B
    }
    let bm: ptr<i32> = rv_bm(rvt, pa)
    let ct: ptr<i32> = rv_ct(rvt, pa)
    bm[j * B + b] = bm[j * B + b] & (0 - (1 << slot) - 1)
    ct[j * B + b] = ct[j * B + b] - 1
}

# Start a transition: allocate the destination generation at 2× (grow) or ½×
# (shrink) the source's bucket count. O(1) — the new arrays are NOT zeroed
# here; that happens incrementally in rv_advance before any copy touches them.
function rv_start_transition(rvt: ptr<RVTable>, kind: i32) -> i32 {
    let src: i32 = rvt[0].epoch
    let dst: i32 = 1 - src
    let Bsrc: i32 = rv_buckets(rvt, src)
    let mut Bdst: i32 = Bsrc * 2
    if kind == 2 {
        Bdst = Bsrc / 2
    }
    if rv_buckets(rvt, dst) > 0 {
        # recycle the idle generation (the initial pre-alloc, or one freed by
        # the previous transition's finish)
        free(rv_bm(rvt, dst))
        free(rv_ct(rvt, dst))
        free(rv_st(rvt, dst))
    }
    let raw_b: ptr<u8> = malloc(RV_CLASSES * Bdst * 4)
    let raw_c: ptr<u8> = malloc(RV_CLASSES * Bdst * 4)
    let raw_s: ptr<u8> = malloc(RV_CLASSES * Bdst * RV_VB * 8)
    let bm: ptr<i32> = raw_b
    let ct: ptr<i32> = raw_c
    let st: ptr<i64> = raw_s
    if dst == 0 {
        rvt[0].bm0 = bm
        rvt[0].ct0 = ct
        rvt[0].st0 = st
        rvt[0].bk0 = Bdst
    } else {
        rvt[0].bm1 = bm
        rvt[0].ct1 = ct
        rvt[0].st1 = st
        rvt[0].bk1 = Bdst
    }
    rvt[0].phase = kind
    rvt[0].cursor = 0
    rvt[0].zero = 0
    return 1
}

# Complete a transition: free the drained generation, flip the epoch. Every
# live pointer now names the new generation.
function rv_finish(rvt: ptr<RVTable>, src: i32) {
    free(rv_bm(rvt, src))
    free(rv_ct(rvt, src))
    free(rv_st(rvt, src))
    if src == 0 {
        rvt[0].bk0 = 0
    } else {
        rvt[0].bk1 = 0
    }
    rvt[0].epoch = 1 - src
    rvt[0].phase = 0
    rvt[0].cursor = 0
    rvt[0].zero = 0
}

# Grow when the live count passes 7·B, shrink when it drops below 3.5·B. The
# bounds are set for the demo's size distribution — the busiest class holds
# ≤ 35% of values — AND for the deamortized transition itself, because inserts
# keep going into the SOURCE generation while the background copy drains it:
# the source's busiest class sits at 0.35·(7·B) = 2.45·B slots/bucket at the
# trigger, and the transition (zeroing + copy at RV_BUDGET per op) can add at
# most ≈ 1.3·B more live values before it completes — so the class never
# exceeds ≈ 2.9 slots/bucket, far below the 8-slot ceiling under
# power-of-two-choices. The shrink floor 3.5·B is the post-shrink grow point
# (7·(B/2) = 3.5·B), so a shrink can never immediately re-trigger a grow.
function rv_maybe_trigger(rvt: ptr<RVTable>, reg: ptr<Registry>) -> i32 {
    if rvt[0].phase != 0 {
        return 0
    }
    let live: i32 = reg[0].live_top[0]
    let B: i32 = rv_buckets(rvt, rvt[0].epoch)
    if live > 7 * B {
        return rv_start_transition(rvt, 1)
    }
    if live < (7 * B) / 2 and B > RV_B_MIN {
        return rv_start_transition(rvt, 2)
    }
    return 0
}

# One deamortized step of an in-flight transition. Returns work units done
# (words zeroed + registry entries examined); -1 on overflow.
function rv_advance(rvt: ptr<RVTable>, reg: ptr<Registry>, vb: ptr<i32>) -> i32 {
    if rvt[0].phase == 0 {
        return 0
    }
    let src: i32 = rvt[0].epoch
    let dst: i32 = 1 - src
    let B: i32 = rv_buckets(rvt, dst)
    let total_words: i32 = RV_CLASSES * B
    let bmd: ptr<i32> = rv_bm(rvt, dst)
    let ctd: ptr<i32> = rv_ct(rvt, dst)
    let mut work: i32 = 0
    # 1. zero the destination's bitmap/count words (before any copy may touch
    #    them, so find_free_bit / two-choice never see garbage)
    let mut z: i32 = 0
    while z < RV_BUDGET and rvt[0].zero < total_words {
        bmd[rvt[0].zero] = 0
        ctd[rvt[0].zero] = 0
        rvt[0].zero = rvt[0].zero + 1
        z = z + 1
    }
    work = work + z
    if rvt[0].zero < total_words {
        return work
    }
    # 2. migrate up to RV_BUDGET live values still in the source generation,
    #    examining at most RV_SCAN registry entries (the paper's per-zone
    #    pointer lists; here the registry sweep plays that role). The sweep is
    #    bounded by the registry high-water mark next_idx — never REG_SIZE —
    #    because mid-transition inserts land at fresh slots that the cursor
    #    must still reach; sweeping past next_idx would strand them in the
    #    freed generation (their gen-bit pointers would dangle).
    let hi: i32 = reg[0].next_idx[0]
    let mut copies: i32 = 0
    let mut scans: i32 = 0
    while copies < RV_BUDGET and scans < RV_SCAN and rvt[0].cursor < hi {
        let idx: i32 = rvt[0].cursor
        rvt[0].cursor = rvt[0].cursor + 1
        scans = scans + 1
        if reg[0].live[idx] == 1 and rv_gen(reg[0].tp[idx]) == src {
            let key: i32 = reg[0].key[idx]
            let bits: i32 = vb[idx]
            let val: i64 = rv_st(rvt, src)[reg[0].pos[idx]]
            let oldtp: i32 = reg[0].tp[idx]
            let mut pos: i32 = 0
            let tp: i32 = rv_alloc(rvt, dst, key, val, bits, &pos)
            if tp < 0 {
                return -1
            }
            # the source slot is abandoned the moment the value is migrated —
            # free it so the source generation's buckets keep only live
            # occupants (otherwise abandoned slots accumulate and inflate the
            # source bucket counts past the 8-slot ceiling mid-transition)
            rv_free(rvt, key, oldtp)
            reg[0].tp[idx] = tp
            reg[0].pos[idx] = pos
            copies = copies + 1
        }
    }
    work = work + scans
    if rvt[0].cursor >= hi {
        rv_finish(rvt, src)
    }
    return work
}

# The naive baseline: finish a transition in one op — zero the whole
# destination and reinsert every live value. Returns work units; -1 overflow.
function rv_complete_now(rvt: ptr<RVTable>, reg: ptr<Registry>, vb: ptr<i32>) -> i32 {
    if rvt[0].phase == 0 {
        return 0
    }
    let src: i32 = rvt[0].epoch
    let dst: i32 = 1 - src
    let B: i32 = rv_buckets(rvt, dst)
    let total_words: i32 = RV_CLASSES * B
    let bmd: ptr<i32> = rv_bm(rvt, dst)
    let ctd: ptr<i32> = rv_ct(rvt, dst)
    let mut work: i32 = 0
    let mut w: i32 = 0
    while w < total_words {
        bmd[w] = 0
        ctd[w] = 0
        w = w + 1
    }
    work = total_words * 2
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < REG_SIZE {
        if reg[0].live[i] == 1 {
            let key: i32 = reg[0].key[i]
            let bits: i32 = vb[i]
            let val: i64 = rv_st(rvt, src)[reg[0].pos[i]]
            let oldtp: i32 = reg[0].tp[i]
            let mut pos: i32 = 0
            let tp: i32 = rv_alloc(rvt, dst, key, val, bits, &pos)
            if tp < 0 {
                return -1
            }
            rv_free(rvt, key, oldtp)
            reg[0].tp[i] = tp
            reg[0].pos[i] = pos
            work = work + 1
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    rv_finish(rvt, src)
    return work
}

# Registry ops for the resizable table — mirror the vdreg_* ops, always
# inserting into the epoch (canonical) generation. New values keep their
# pointers in the epoch generation during a transition; the background
# migration moves them to the destination before the epoch flips.
function rvreg_insert(rvt: ptr<RVTable>, reg: ptr<Registry>, vv: ptr<i64>,
                      vb: ptr<i32>, key: i32, value: i64, bits: i32) -> i32 {
    let idx: i32 = reg[0].next_idx[0]
    if idx >= REG_SIZE {
        return 0
    }
    let mut pos: i32 = 0
    let tp: i32 = rv_alloc(rvt, rvt[0].epoch, key, value, bits, &pos)
    if tp < 0 {
        return 0
    }
    reg[0].next_idx[0] = idx + 1
    reg[0].key[idx] = key
    vv[idx] = value
    vb[idx] = bits
    reg[0].tp[idx] = tp
    reg[0].pos[idx] = pos
    reg[0].live[idx] = 1
    let top: i32 = reg[0].live_top[0]
    reg[0].live_list[top] = idx
    reg[0].live_top[0] = top + 1
    return 1
}

function rvreg_delete_random(rvt: ptr<RVTable>, reg: ptr<Registry>,
                             state: ptr<i32>) -> i32 {
    let top: i32 = reg[0].live_top[0]
    if top == 0 {
        return 0
    }
    let j: i32 = rng_next(state) % top
    let idx: i32 = reg[0].live_list[j]
    let key: i32 = reg[0].key[idx]
    let tp: i32 = reg[0].tp[idx]
    rv_free(rvt, key, tp)
    reg[0].live[idx] = 0
    reg[0].tp[idx] = -1
    reg[0].live_list[j] = reg[0].live_list[top - 1]
    reg[0].live_top[0] = top - 1
    return 1
}

# Verify EVERY live key across both generations (each pointer names its own).
function rvreg_verify(rvt: ptr<RVTable>, reg: ptr<Registry>, vv: ptr<i64>,
                      vb: ptr<i32>) -> i32 {
    let mut errors: i32 = 0
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < REG_SIZE {
        if reg[0].live[i] == 1 {
            let tp: i32 = reg[0].tp[i]
            let pos: i32 = rv_deref(rvt, reg[0].key[i], tp)
            if pos != reg[0].pos[i] {
                errors = errors + 1
            }
            if pos == reg[0].pos[i] and rv_st(rvt, rv_gen(tp))[pos] != vv[i] {
                errors = errors + 1
            }
            if vd_cap((tp >> 4) & 7) < vb[i] {
                errors = errors + 1
            }
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return errors
}

# Per-generation bucket invariant: no bucket over RV_VB slots. During an
# in-flight transition only the ZEROED prefix of the destination generation is
# valid — the incremental rebuild zeroes it RV_BUDGET words per op, so the
# unbuilt tail is uninitialized heap (never a real overfull bucket; the
# allocator's find_free_bit ceiling is what actually bounds every live slot).
# Scope the scan to rvt[0].zero for the destination while phase != 0.
function rvtable_check(rvt: ptr<RVTable>) -> i32 {
    let mut errors: i32 = 0
    let mut pa: i32 = 0
    while pa < 2 {
        let B: i32 = rv_buckets(rvt, pa)
        if B > 0 {
            let ct: ptr<i32> = rv_ct(rvt, pa)
            let limit: i32 = RV_CLASSES * B
            let mut valid: i32 = limit
            if rvt[0].phase != 0 and pa != rvt[0].epoch {
                # in-flight rebuild: only the zeroed prefix is initialized
                valid = rvt[0].zero
                if valid > limit {
                    valid = limit
                }
            }
            let mut w: i32 = 0
            while w < valid {
                if ct[w] > RV_VB {
                    errors = errors + 1
                }
                w = w + 1
            }
        }
        pa = pa + 1
    }
    return errors
}

# Run the Phase-14 workload on the table and registry: load → churn → drain →
# reload. `mode` 1 = naive one-shot resizing (finish each transition in the
# op that triggers it), 2 = deamortized (RV_BUDGET per op). Reports the
# worst-case per-op cost (work units, wall ns) via out-params; block verifies
# happen OUTSIDE the per-op timing window so no op is polluted by a Θ(n) scan.
function rv_run_workload(rvt: ptr<RVTable>, reg: ptr<Registry>, vv: ptr<i64>,
                         vb: ptr<i32>, state: ptr<i32>, mode: i32,
                         mx_work: ptr<i32>, mx_ns: ptr<i64>, errs: ptr<i32>) {
    let mut next_key: i32 = 0
    let scratch_raw: ptr<u8> = malloc(50000 + state[0] % 350000)
    free(scratch_raw)
    # ---- load (3 resize grows) ----
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < RV_LOAD {
        let key: i32 = distinct_key(next_key)
        let mut bits: i32 = 0
        let value: i64 = vd_make_value(state, key, &bits)
        let op0: i64 = now_ns()
        let ok: i32 = rvreg_insert(rvt, reg, vv, vb, key, value, bits)
        if ok == 0 {
            errs[0] = errs[0] + 1
        }
        let mut work: i32 = 1
        work = work + rv_maybe_trigger(rvt, reg)
        if rvt[0].phase != 0 {
            if mode == 1 {
                let cw: i32 = rv_complete_now(rvt, reg, vb)
                if cw < 0 {
                    errs[0] = errs[0] + 1
                }
                work = work + cw
            } else {
                let aw: i32 = rv_advance(rvt, reg, vb)
                if aw < 0 {
                    errs[0] = errs[0] + 1
                }
                work = work + aw
            }
        }
        let op1: i64 = now_ns()
        if work > mx_work[0] {
            mx_work[0] = work
        }
        let d: i64 = op1 - op0
        if d > mx_ns[0] {
            mx_ns[0] = d
        }
        next_key = next_key + 1
        i = i + 1
    }
    errs[0] = errs[0] + rvreg_verify(rvt, reg, vv, vb)
    errs[0] = errs[0] + rvtable_check(rvt)
    # ---- churn (55/45 insert/delete) ----
    let mut ops: i32 = 0
    while ops < RV_CHURN {
        let op0: i64 = now_ns()
        let rr: i32 = rng_next(state) % 100
        if rr < 55 and reg[0].live_top[0] < REG_SIZE - 4 {
            let key: i32 = distinct_key(next_key)
            let mut bits: i32 = 0
            let value: i64 = vd_make_value(state, key, &bits)
            let ok: i32 = rvreg_insert(rvt, reg, vv, vb, key, value, bits)
            if ok == 0 {
                errs[0] = errs[0] + 1
            }
            next_key = next_key + 1
        } else {
            let _d: i32 = rvreg_delete_random(rvt, reg, state)
        }
        let mut work: i32 = 1
        work = work + rv_maybe_trigger(rvt, reg)
        if rvt[0].phase != 0 {
            if mode == 1 {
                let cw: i32 = rv_complete_now(rvt, reg, vb)
                if cw < 0 {
                    errs[0] = errs[0] + 1
                }
                work = work + cw
            } else {
                let aw: i32 = rv_advance(rvt, reg, vb)
                if aw < 0 {
                    errs[0] = errs[0] + 1
                }
                work = work + aw
            }
        }
        let op1: i64 = now_ns()
        if work > mx_work[0] {
            mx_work[0] = work
        }
        let d: i64 = op1 - op0
        if d > mx_ns[0] {
            mx_ns[0] = d
        }
        ops = ops + 1
    }
    errs[0] = errs[0] + rvreg_verify(rvt, reg, vv, vb)
    errs[0] = errs[0] + rvtable_check(rvt)
    # ---- drain (delete-heavy — the 3 shrinks) ----
    let mut di: i32 = 0
    while di < RV_DRAIN {
        let op0: i64 = now_ns()
        let _d: i32 = rvreg_delete_random(rvt, reg, state)
        let mut work: i32 = 1
        work = work + rv_maybe_trigger(rvt, reg)
        if rvt[0].phase != 0 {
            if mode == 1 {
                let cw: i32 = rv_complete_now(rvt, reg, vb)
                if cw < 0 {
                    errs[0] = errs[0] + 1
                }
                work = work + cw
            } else {
                let aw: i32 = rv_advance(rvt, reg, vb)
                if aw < 0 {
                    errs[0] = errs[0] + 1
                }
                work = work + aw
            }
        }
        let op1: i64 = now_ns()
        if work > mx_work[0] {
            mx_work[0] = work
        }
        let d: i64 = op1 - op0
        if d > mx_ns[0] {
            mx_ns[0] = d
        }
        di = di + 1
    }
    errs[0] = errs[0] + rvreg_verify(rvt, reg, vv, vb)
    errs[0] = errs[0] + rvtable_check(rvt)
    # ---- reload (grow-after-shrink) ----
    let mut ri: i32 = 0
    while ri < RV_RELOAD {
        let key: i32 = distinct_key(next_key)
        let mut bits: i32 = 0
        let value: i64 = vd_make_value(state, key, &bits)
        let op0: i64 = now_ns()
        let ok: i32 = rvreg_insert(rvt, reg, vv, vb, key, value, bits)
        if ok == 0 {
            errs[0] = errs[0] + 1
        }
        let mut work: i32 = 1
        work = work + rv_maybe_trigger(rvt, reg)
        if rvt[0].phase != 0 {
            if mode == 1 {
                let cw: i32 = rv_complete_now(rvt, reg, vb)
                if cw < 0 {
                    errs[0] = errs[0] + 1
                }
                work = work + cw
            } else {
                let aw: i32 = rv_advance(rvt, reg, vb)
                if aw < 0 {
                    errs[0] = errs[0] + 1
                }
                work = work + aw
            }
        }
        let op1: i64 = now_ns()
        if work > mx_work[0] {
            mx_work[0] = work
        }
        let d: i64 = op1 - op0
        if d > mx_ns[0] {
            mx_ns[0] = d
        }
        next_key = next_key + 1
        ri = ri + 1
    }
    errs[0] = errs[0] + rvreg_verify(rvt, reg, vv, vb)
    errs[0] = errs[0] + rvtable_check(rvt)
    # flush any in-flight transition (unmeasured warm-down, same O(1) budget)
    if rvt[0].phase != 0 {
        if mode == 1 {
            let cw: i32 = rv_complete_now(rvt, reg, vb)
            if cw < 0 {
                errs[0] = errs[0] + 1
            }
        } else {
            while rvt[0].phase != 0 {
                let aw: i32 = rv_advance(rvt, reg, vb)
                if aw < 0 {
                    errs[0] = errs[0] + 1
                }
            }
        }
    }
    errs[0] = errs[0] + rvreg_verify(rvt, reg, vv, vb)
    errs[0] = errs[0] + rvtable_check(rvt)
    if rvt[0].phase != 0 {
        errs[0] = errs[0] + 1
    }
}

# ============================================================================
# Theorem 9 (proof) — r-LEVELS OF INDIRECTION (paper Section 6.5)
#
# The size-class table caps values at O(1) machine words because its class
# slots are sized to the VALUE: a 200-bit value would need a 256-bit slot in
# the base table, and the base class tag would have to grow with the max
# value size. The paper's fix: store every value at the end of a linked list
# of length O(1) — 'value k bits ← p₁ of size O(log k) bits ← p₂ of size
# O(log log k) bits ← … ← base pointer of size O(log⁽ʳ⁾ n) bits', where each
# pointer is exponentially larger than the one it points at.
#
# Phase 15 lifts the v ≤ 64 cap with r = 2 levels:
#   • values ≤ 64 bits  — stored DIRECTLY in the base VDTable (classes 0-6),
#     exactly as Phase 11; the base pointer is (flag 0 + class + choice + slot);
#   • values of 65-256 bits — stored in the LEVEL-1 ARENA (a compact
#     power-of-two-choices table with 128-bit and 256-bit classes, keyed on
#     the key like every other tiny-pointer table); the base pointer (flag 1)
#     names an 8-bit slot in base class RL_PTR_CLASS holding p₁ = 5 bits:
#     (1 arena-class bit + 1 choice bit + 3 slot bits) = O(log 256);
#   • the base pointer NEVER grows with the value size — 8 bits for v = 1..256
#     — the paper's 'base pointer … is O(log⁽ʳ⁾ n) bits', and the base table's
#     geometry is independent of value size; only the compact arena grows.
#
# Pointer metadata (the paper's O(j) extra bits): bit 7 of the base pointer
# says 'points at another pointer vs a final value' (a); the class bits say
# the size of the thing pointed at (b) — class j for direct values, the
# holder class + p₁'s arena-class bit for chained values.
#
# Space accounting (per live key, Phase-11 convention):
#   uniform-256 baseline:           256 bits
#   single-level size-classed:      8 (pointer) + 2^⌈log v⌉ (one base slot)
#   r-levels chain:                 8 (pointer) + 2^⌈log v⌉ (payload; big
#                                   ones in the arena) + 13 per BIG value
#                                   (8-bit holder slot + 5-bit p₁)
# The 13 bits/value on big values only is exactly the paper's O(log v)
# per-value pointer overhead (Eq. 4) — the price of one level of indirection.

struct RLTable {
    bm: ptr<i32>,    # 2 × RL_ARENA_B occupancy words (classes 0,1)
    ct: ptr<i32>,    # 2 × RL_ARENA_B per-bucket counts
    st: ptr<i64>     # 2 × RL_ARENA_B × VD_VB slots × 4 words (256-bit slots)
}

function rltable_create() -> RLTable {
    let raw_b: ptr<u8> = malloc(2 * RL_ARENA_B * 4)
    let raw_c: ptr<u8> = malloc(2 * RL_ARENA_B * 4)
    let raw_s: ptr<u8> = malloc(2 * RL_ARENA_B * VD_VB * 4 * 8)
    let bm: ptr<i32> = raw_b
    let ct: ptr<i32> = raw_c
    let st: ptr<i64> = raw_s
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < 2 * RL_ARENA_B {
        bm[i] = 0
        ct[i] = 0
        i = i + 1
    }
    i = 0
    while i < 2 * RL_ARENA_B * VD_VB * 4 {
        st[i] = 0
        i = i + 1
    }
    return RLTable { bm: bm, ct: ct, st: st }
}

function rltable_destroy(rlt: ptr<RLTable>) {
    free(rlt[0].bm)
    free(rlt[0].ct)
    free(rlt[0].st)
}

# Low n bits of an i64 as a mask (n in [0, 256]; clamps at 64).
function rl_mask(n: i32) -> i64 {
    if n <= 0 {
        return 0
    }
    if n >= 64 {
        return -1
    }
    return ((1 as i64) << n) - 1
}

# Arena class: 0 = 128-bit slots, 1 = 256-bit slots.
function rl_arena_class(bits: i32) -> i32 {
    if bits <= 128 {
        return 0
    }
    return 1
}

# Allocate a 128/256-bit value (four i64 words, masked to `bits`) into the
# level-1 arena. Returns the 5-bit p₁ (>= 0), or -1 if both buckets are full.
# Writes the absolute WORD offset of the value to pos_out (for verify).
function rl_allocate(rlt: ptr<RLTable>, key: i32, w0: i64, w1: i64, w2: i64,
                     w3: i64, bits: i32, pos_out: ptr<i32>) -> i32 {
    let a: i32 = rl_arena_class(bits)
    let b1: i32 = hash_u32(key, 51 + a) % RL_ARENA_B
    let b2: i32 = hash_u32(key, 61 + a) % RL_ARENA_B
    let mut pick: i32 = b1
    let mut choice: i32 = 0
    if rlt[0].ct[a * RL_ARENA_B + b2] < rlt[0].ct[a * RL_ARENA_B + b1] {
        pick = b2
        choice = 1
    }
    let slot: i32 = find_free_bit(rlt[0].bm[a * RL_ARENA_B + pick], VD_VB)
    if slot < 0 {
        return -1
    }
    rlt[0].bm[a * RL_ARENA_B + pick] = rlt[0].bm[a * RL_ARENA_B + pick] | (1 << slot)
    rlt[0].ct[a * RL_ARENA_B + pick] = rlt[0].ct[a * RL_ARENA_B + pick] + 1
    let pos: i32 = ((a * RL_ARENA_B + pick) * VD_VB + slot) * 4
    rlt[0].st[pos] = w0
    rlt[0].st[pos + 1] = w1
    rlt[0].st[pos + 2] = w2
    rlt[0].st[pos + 3] = w3
    pos_out[0] = pos
    return (a << 4) | (choice << 3) | slot
}

# Dereference p₁: the key supplies the arena bucket, p₁ the class/choice/slot.
# Returns the absolute WORD offset of the value.
function rl_deref(rlt: ptr<RLTable>, key: i32, p1: i32) -> i32 {
    let a: i32 = (p1 >> 4) & 1
    let choice: i32 = (p1 >> 3) & 1
    let slot: i32 = p1 & 7
    let mut b: i32 = hash_u32(key, 51 + a) % RL_ARENA_B
    if choice == 1 {
        b = hash_u32(key, 61 + a) % RL_ARENA_B
    }
    return ((a * RL_ARENA_B + b) * VD_VB + slot) * 4
}

function rl_free(rlt: ptr<RLTable>, key: i32, p1: i32) {
    let a: i32 = (p1 >> 4) & 1
    let choice: i32 = (p1 >> 3) & 1
    let slot: i32 = p1 & 7
    let mut b: i32 = hash_u32(key, 51 + a) % RL_ARENA_B
    if choice == 1 {
        b = hash_u32(key, 61 + a) % RL_ARENA_B
    }
    rlt[0].bm[a * RL_ARENA_B + b] = rlt[0].bm[a * RL_ARENA_B + b] & (0 - (1 << slot) - 1)
    rlt[0].ct[a * RL_ARENA_B + b] = rlt[0].ct[a * RL_ARENA_B + b] - 1
}

# Arena invariant: no bucket may exceed VD_VB slots. Returns error count.
function rltable_check(rlt: ptr<RLTable>) -> i32 {
    let mut errors: i32 = 0
    let mut a: i32 = 0
    while a < 2 {
        let mut b: i32 = 0
        while b < RL_ARENA_B {
            if rlt[0].ct[a * RL_ARENA_B + b] > VD_VB {
                errors = errors + 1
            }
            b = b + 1
        }
        a = a + 1
    }
    return errors
}

# ---- registry operations for the r-levels dictionary -----------------------
# Reuses Registry for key/tp/pos/live; parallel arrays rw0..rw3 (i64 value
# words, bit-masked per key), rlb (bit length), rl1 (p₁, -1 for direct),
# rla (arena word offset, -1 for direct).

function rlreg_insert(reg: ptr<Registry>, base: ptr<VDTable>, rlt: ptr<RLTable>,
                      rw0: ptr<i64>, rw1: ptr<i64>, rw2: ptr<i64>, rw3: ptr<i64>,
                      rlb: ptr<i32>, rl1: ptr<i32>, rla: ptr<i32>,
                      key: i32, w0: i64, w1: i64, w2: i64, w3: i64,
                      bits: i32) -> i32 {
    let idx: i32 = reg[0].next_idx[0]
    if idx >= REG_SIZE {
        return 0
    }
    let mut pos: i32 = 0
    let mut tp: i32 = 0
    let mut p1: i32 = -1
    let mut apos: i32 = -1
    if bits < RL_BIG {
        tp = vd_allocate(base, key, w0, bits, &pos)
        if tp < 0 {
            return 0
        }
    } else {
        p1 = rl_allocate(rlt, key, w0, w1, w2, w3, bits, &apos)
        if p1 < 0 {
            return 0
        }
        # base slot (class RL_PTR_CLASS, 8-bit) holds p₁; flag bit 7 = chained
        tp = vd_allocate(base, key, p1, 5, &pos)
        if tp < 0 {
            rl_free(rlt, key, p1)
            return 0
        }
        tp = (1 << 7) | tp
    }
    reg[0].next_idx[0] = idx + 1
    reg[0].key[idx] = key
    reg[0].tp[idx] = tp
    reg[0].pos[idx] = pos
    reg[0].live[idx] = 1
    rw0[idx] = w0
    rw1[idx] = w1
    rw2[idx] = w2
    rw3[idx] = w3
    rlb[idx] = bits
    rl1[idx] = p1
    rla[idx] = apos
    let top: i32 = reg[0].live_top[0]
    reg[0].live_list[top] = idx
    reg[0].live_top[0] = top + 1
    return 1
}

function rlreg_delete_random(reg: ptr<Registry>, base: ptr<VDTable>,
                             rlt: ptr<RLTable>, rl1: ptr<i32>,
                             state: ptr<i32>) -> i32 {
    let top: i32 = reg[0].live_top[0]
    if top == 0 {
        return 0
    }
    let j: i32 = rng_next(state) % top
    let idx: i32 = reg[0].live_list[j]
    let key: i32 = reg[0].key[idx]
    let tp: i32 = reg[0].tp[idx]
    if ((tp >> 7) & 1) == 1 {
        rl_free(rlt, key, rl1[idx])
    }
    vd_free(base, key, tp)
    reg[0].live[idx] = 0
    reg[0].tp[idx] = -1
    reg[0].live_list[j] = reg[0].live_list[top - 1]
    reg[0].live_top[0] = top - 1
    return 1
}

# Verify EVERY live key through the chain: direct values read back from the
# base table; chained values read p₁ from the base slot, then the value words
# from the arena. All 4 words are compared bit-exactly (masked per `bits`).
function rlreg_verify(reg: ptr<Registry>, rw0: ptr<i64>, rw1: ptr<i64>,
                      rw2: ptr<i64>, rw3: ptr<i64>, rlb: ptr<i32>,
                      rl1: ptr<i32>, rla: ptr<i32>,
                      base: ptr<VDTable>, rlt: ptr<RLTable>) -> i32 {
    let mut errors: i32 = 0
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < REG_SIZE {
        if reg[0].live[i] == 1 {
            let key: i32 = reg[0].key[i]
            let tp: i32 = reg[0].tp[i]
            let bits: i32 = rlb[i]
            let pos: i32 = vd_dereference(base, key, tp)
            if pos != reg[0].pos[i] {
                errors = errors + 1
            }
            if ((tp >> 7) & 1) == 0 {
                # direct value: the base slot holds the value itself
                if pos == reg[0].pos[i] and base[0].store[pos] != rw0[i] {
                    errors = errors + 1
                }
                if vd_cap((tp >> 4) & 7) < bits {
                    errors = errors + 1
                }
            } else {
                # chained: the base slot holds p₁; the value lives in the arena
                let p1: i32 = rl1[i]
                if pos == reg[0].pos[i] and base[0].store[pos] != (p1 as i64) {
                    errors = errors + 1
                }
                if ((tp >> 4) & 7) != RL_PTR_CLASS {
                    errors = errors + 1      # holder must sit in the 8-bit class
                }
                let apos: i32 = rl_deref(rlt, key, p1)
                if apos != rla[i] {
                    errors = errors + 1
                }
                if apos == rla[i] {
                    if rlt[0].st[apos] != rw0[i] {
                        errors = errors + 1
                    }
                    if rlt[0].st[apos + 1] != rw1[i] {
                        errors = errors + 1
                    }
                    if rlt[0].st[apos + 2] != rw2[i] {
                        errors = errors + 1
                    }
                    if rlt[0].st[apos + 3] != rw3[i] {
                        errors = errors + 1
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return errors
}

# Skewed value-size draw INCLUDING big values, with a deterministic 256-bit
# value (4 hashed words, each masked to the bit budget of that word).
function rl_make_words(state: ptr<i32>, key: i32, bits_out: ptr<i32>,
                       w0: ptr<i64>, w1: ptr<i64>, w2: ptr<i64>, w3: ptr<i64>) {
    let r: i32 = rng_next(state) % 100
    let mut bits: i32 = 0
    if r < 60 {
        bits = 1 + (rng_next(state) % 8)          # 1..8
    } else {
        if r < 78 {
            bits = 9 + (rng_next(state) % 8)      # 9..16
        } else {
            if r < 90 {
                bits = 17 + (rng_next(state) % 48)  # 17..64
            } else {
                if r < 97 {
                    bits = 65 + (rng_next(state) % 64)   # 65..128 (arena class 0)
                } else {
                    bits = 129 + (rng_next(state) % 128) # 129..256 (arena class 1)
                }
            }
        }
    }
    bits_out[0] = bits
    w0[0] = (hash_u32(key, 90) as i64) & rl_mask(bits)
    w1[0] = (hash_u32(key, 91) as i64) & rl_mask(bits - 64)
    w2[0] = (hash_u32(key, 92) as i64) & rl_mask(bits - 128)
    w3[0] = (hash_u32(key, 93) as i64) & rl_mask(bits - 192)
}

# ============================================================================
# Phase 12 key chain — VARIABLE-SIZE KEYS through the r-levels chain
# (Theorem 9's keys analogue, applied to the KEY array exactly as Phase 15
# applies it to values: keys of 65-256 bits live in a compact KEY ARENA behind
# a 5-bit p₁ held in an 8-bit base class-3 holder slot, flag bit 7 = chained;
# keys ≤ 64 bits store directly in the base key VDTable. The base pointer
# stays 8 bits for EVERY key size — the keys analogue of Phase 15's
# base-pointer independence.)
#
# Registry arrays per pair: reg.tp/reg.pos = key base pointer/position,
# kw0..kw3 = the four expected key words (only kw0 used for direct keys),
# kb = key bit-length, kc1 = p₁ (-1 direct), kca = arena word offset (-1
# direct); value side: vtp/vpos, vv/vb as in Phase 12.
# ============================================================================

# Skewed KEY-size draw INCLUDING big keys (mirrors rl_make_words: 60% ≤ 8,
# 18% ≤ 16, 12% ≤ 64, 7% 65-128, 3% 129-256) + deterministic 256-bit key
# payload (4 hashed words masked per bit budget; salts 94-97 keep key words
# distinct from the value words of salts 90-93).
function kch_make_key(state: ptr<i32>, key: i32, bits_out: ptr<i32>,
                      w0: ptr<i64>, w1: ptr<i64>, w2: ptr<i64>, w3: ptr<i64>) {
    let r: i32 = rng_next(state) % 100
    let mut bits: i32 = 0
    if r < 60 {
        bits = 1 + (rng_next(state) % 8)
    } else {
        if r < 78 {
            bits = 9 + (rng_next(state) % 8)
        } else {
            if r < 90 {
                bits = 17 + (rng_next(state) % 48)
            } else {
                if r < 97 {
                    bits = 65 + (rng_next(state) % 64)
                } else {
                    bits = 129 + (rng_next(state) % 128)
                }
            }
        }
    }
    bits_out[0] = bits
    w0[0] = (hash_u32(key, 94) as i64) & rl_mask(bits)
    w1[0] = (hash_u32(key, 95) as i64) & rl_mask(bits - 64)
    w2[0] = (hash_u32(key, 96) as i64) & rl_mask(bits - 128)
    w3[0] = (hash_u32(key, 97) as i64) & rl_mask(bits - 192)
}

# Insert one (key, value) pair: the KEY side goes through the chain (direct in
# kvt when keybits < KDK_BIG, else arena + 8-bit holder with flag bit 7); the
# VALUE side stores directly in vvt exactly as Phase 12. Rollback on failure
# frees every side already allocated.
function kchreg_insert(reg: ptr<Registry>, kvt: ptr<VDTable>, krlt: ptr<RLTable>,
                       vvt: ptr<VDTable>,
                       kw0: ptr<i64>, kw1: ptr<i64>, kw2: ptr<i64>, kw3: ptr<i64>,
                       kb: ptr<i32>, kc1: ptr<i32>, kca: ptr<i32>,
                       vtp: ptr<i32>, vpos: ptr<i32>, vv: ptr<i64>, vb: ptr<i32>,
                       key: i32, keybits: i32, k0: i64, k1: i64, k2: i64, k3: i64,
                       valbits: i32, valpay: i64) -> i32 {
    let idx: i32 = reg[0].next_idx[0]
    if idx >= REG_SIZE {
        return 0
    }
    let mut kp: i32 = 0
    let mut ktp: i32 = 0
    let mut p1: i32 = -1
    let mut kap: i32 = -1
    if keybits < KDK_BIG {
        ktp = vd_allocate(kvt, key, k0, keybits, &kp)
        if ktp < 0 {
            return 0
        }
    } else {
        p1 = rl_allocate(krlt, key, k0, k1, k2, k3, keybits, &kap)
        if p1 < 0 {
            return 0
        }
        # base holder slot (class KDK_PTR_CLASS, 8-bit) holds p₁; flag bit 7
        ktp = vd_allocate(kvt, key, p1, 5, &kp)
        if ktp < 0 {
            rl_free(krlt, key, p1)
            return 0
        }
        ktp = (1 << 7) | ktp
    }
    let mut vp: i32 = 0
    let vtp2: i32 = vd_allocate(vvt, key, valpay, valbits, &vp)
    if vtp2 < 0 {
        if ((ktp >> 7) & 1) == 1 {
            rl_free(krlt, key, p1)
        }
        vd_free(kvt, key, ktp)
        return 0
    }
    reg[0].next_idx[0] = idx + 1
    reg[0].key[idx] = key
    reg[0].tp[idx] = ktp
    reg[0].pos[idx] = kp
    reg[0].live[idx] = 1
    kw0[idx] = k0
    kw1[idx] = k1
    kw2[idx] = k2
    kw3[idx] = k3
    kb[idx] = keybits
    kc1[idx] = p1
    kca[idx] = kap
    vtp[idx] = vtp2
    vpos[idx] = vp
    vv[idx] = valpay
    vb[idx] = valbits
    let top: i32 = reg[0].live_top[0]
    reg[0].live_list[top] = idx
    reg[0].live_top[0] = top + 1
    return 1
}

# Delete a uniformly random live pair: free the key side (arena slot if
# chained, then the base slot) and the value slot.
function kchreg_delete_random(reg: ptr<Registry>, kvt: ptr<VDTable>,
                              krlt: ptr<RLTable>, vvt: ptr<VDTable>,
                              kc1: ptr<i32>, vtp: ptr<i32>,
                              state: ptr<i32>) -> i32 {
    let top: i32 = reg[0].live_top[0]
    if top == 0 {
        return 0
    }
    let j: i32 = rng_next(state) % top
    let idx: i32 = reg[0].live_list[j]
    let key: i32 = reg[0].key[idx]
    let ktp: i32 = reg[0].tp[idx]
    if ((ktp >> 7) & 1) == 1 {
        rl_free(krlt, key, kc1[idx])
    }
    vd_free(kvt, key, ktp)
    vd_free(vvt, key, vtp[idx])
    reg[0].live[idx] = 0
    reg[0].tp[idx] = -1
    reg[0].live_list[j] = reg[0].live_list[top - 1]
    reg[0].live_top[0] = top - 1
    return 1
}

# Verify EVERY live pair through the key chain: the key words read back from
# the base table (direct) or the key arena (chained) are compared bit-exactly,
# plus the value round-trip and both pointer-class fits.
function kchreg_verify(reg: ptr<Registry>, kvt: ptr<VDTable>, krlt: ptr<RLTable>,
                       vvt: ptr<VDTable>,
                       kw0: ptr<i64>, kw1: ptr<i64>, kw2: ptr<i64>, kw3: ptr<i64>,
                       kb: ptr<i32>, kc1: ptr<i32>, kca: ptr<i32>,
                       vtp: ptr<i32>, vpos: ptr<i32>, vv: ptr<i64>, vb: ptr<i32>) -> i32 {
    let mut errors: i32 = 0
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < REG_SIZE {
        if reg[0].live[i] == 1 {
            let key: i32 = reg[0].key[i]
            let ktp: i32 = reg[0].tp[i]
            let keybits: i32 = kb[i]
            let kp: i32 = vd_dereference(kvt, key, ktp)
            if kp != reg[0].pos[i] {
                errors = errors + 1
            }
            if ((ktp >> 7) & 1) == 0 {
                # direct key: the base slot holds word 0 itself
                if kp == reg[0].pos[i] and kvt[0].store[kp] != kw0[i] {
                    errors = errors + 1
                }
                if vd_cap((ktp >> 4) & 7) < keybits {
                    errors = errors + 1
                }
            } else {
                # chained key: the base slot holds p₁; the key lives in the arena
                let p1: i32 = kc1[i]
                if kp == reg[0].pos[i] and kvt[0].store[kp] != (p1 as i64) {
                    errors = errors + 1
                }
                if ((ktp >> 4) & 7) != KDK_PTR_CLASS {
                    errors = errors + 1      # holder must sit in the 8-bit class
                }
                let apos: i32 = rl_deref(krlt, key, p1)
                if apos != kca[i] {
                    errors = errors + 1
                }
                if apos == kca[i] {
                    if krlt[0].st[apos] != kw0[i] {
                        errors = errors + 1
                    }
                    if krlt[0].st[apos + 1] != kw1[i] {
                        errors = errors + 1
                    }
                    if krlt[0].st[apos + 2] != kw2[i] {
                        errors = errors + 1
                    }
                    if krlt[0].st[apos + 3] != kw3[i] {
                        errors = errors + 1
                    }
                }
            }
            # value side (always direct, exactly as Phase 12)
            let vp: i32 = vd_dereference(vvt, key, vtp[i])
            if vp != vpos[i] {
                errors = errors + 1
            }
            if vp == vpos[i] and vvt[0].store[vp] != vv[i] {
                errors = errors + 1
            }
            if vd_cap((vtp[i] >> 4) & 7) < vb[i] {
                errors = errors + 1
            }
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return errors
}

# Key-side arena invariant (bucket occupancy ≤ VD_VB) — the base key table's
# invariants are covered by vdtable_check.
function kchtable_check(krlt: ptr<RLTable>) -> i32 {
    return rltable_check(krlt)
}

# ============================================================================
# --- Phase 15 (r = 3 extension): base(8) → p₂(6) → p₁(5) → value (2^32 bits) --
# ============================================================================
#
# The r=2 chain caps values at 256 bits because its arena slots are four i64
# words. The paper's r=3 chain inserts the O(log log k) middle pointer p₂:
#
#   value k bits ← p₁ of O(log k) bits ← p₂ of O(log log k) bits ← base of
#   O(log⁽³⁾ n) bits   ('every value is stored at the end of a linked list of
#    length O(1), where the base pointer … is O(log⁽ʳ⁾ n) bits, and each
#    subsequent pointer is exponentially larger than the previous one')
#
# Concrete instance:
#   • base pointer = 8 bits, UNCHANGED: flag(1) + class(3) + choice(1) + slot(3)
#     — the class-3 base slot now holds p₂ instead of p₁. The base never grows
#     with the value size, even for v = 2^32 bits (the paper's base-pointer
#     independence: O(log⁽³⁾ n), constant in k).
#   • p₂ = 6 bits = class(1) + choice(1) + slot(4) — points into the LEVEL-2
#     table (2 classes × RL3_P2_B buckets × 16 slots) that holds the 5-bit p₁.
#   • p₁ = 5 bits = class(1) + choice(1) + slot(3) — points into the LEVEL-1
#     ARENA (2 classes × RL3_ARENA_B buckets × 8 slots); the arena slot names
#     an offset into a SHARED WORD POOL, so a slot holds a value of ANY width
#     (up to 2^32 bits in the design; the demo stores up to 2^20 to stay fast).
#   • measured pointer hierarchy: 8 → 6 → 5 bits (base → p₂ → p₁), with the
#     base CONSTANT — the chain's per-big-value cost rises from r=2's 13 bits
#     (8-bit holder + 5-bit p₁) to r=3's 19 bits (8-bit holder + 6-bit p₂ +
#     5-bit p₁) while the value cap lifts from 256 bits to 2^32 bits. These
#     widths are GEOMETRY CONSTANTS (class + choice + slot bits of each table),
#     not value-size-dependent: the demo captures the paper's base-pointer
#     independence, not its exponentially-growing pointer hierarchy.
#
# Value words are deterministic functions of (key, word index), so verification
# regenerates and compares them bit-exactly without storing the words.
# ============================================================================

struct R3Arena {
    bm: ptr<i32>,     # 2 × RL3_ARENA_B occupancy words (8 bits used)
    ct: ptr<i32>,     # 2 × RL3_ARENA_B per-bucket counts
    soff: ptr<i32>,   # 2 × RL3_ARENA_B × 8 slots — word offset into the pool
    pool: ptr<i64>,   # RL3_POOL_WORDS shared value storage (bump-allocated:
                      #   freed words are NOT reclaimed — the demo's churn is
    fill: ptr<i32>    #   sized so the bump never exhausts RL3_POOL_WORDS)
}

struct R3P2 {
    bm: ptr<i32>,     # 2 × RL3_P2_B occupancy words (16 bits used)
    ct: ptr<i32>,     # 2 × RL3_P2_B per-bucket counts
    p1: ptr<i32>      # 2 × RL3_P2_B × 16 slots — the 5-bit p₁ (-1 = free)
}

function r3a_create() -> R3Arena {
    let raw_b: ptr<u8> = malloc(2 * RL3_ARENA_B * 4)
    let raw_c: ptr<u8> = malloc(2 * RL3_ARENA_B * 4)
    let raw_s: ptr<u8> = malloc(2 * RL3_ARENA_B * 8 * 4)
    let raw_p: ptr<u8> = malloc(RL3_POOL_WORDS * 8)
    let raw_f: ptr<u8> = malloc(4)
    let bm: ptr<i32> = raw_b
    let ct: ptr<i32> = raw_c
    let soff: ptr<i32> = raw_s
    let pool: ptr<i64> = raw_p
    let fill: ptr<i32> = raw_f
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < 2 * RL3_ARENA_B {
        bm[i] = 0
        ct[i] = 0
        i = i + 1
    }
    i = 0
    while i < 2 * RL3_ARENA_B * 8 {
        soff[i] = 0
        i = i + 1
    }
    i = 0
    while i < RL3_POOL_WORDS {
        pool[i] = 0
        i = i + 1
    }
    fill[0] = 0
    return R3Arena { bm: bm, ct: ct, soff: soff, pool: pool, fill: fill }
}

function r3a_destroy(a: ptr<R3Arena>) {
    free(a[0].bm)
    free(a[0].ct)
    free(a[0].soff)
    free(a[0].pool)
    free(a[0].fill)
}

function r3p2_create() -> R3P2 {
    let raw_b: ptr<u8> = malloc(2 * RL3_P2_B * 4)
    let raw_c: ptr<u8> = malloc(2 * RL3_P2_B * 4)
    let raw_s: ptr<u8> = malloc(2 * RL3_P2_B * 16 * 4)
    let bm: ptr<i32> = raw_b
    let ct: ptr<i32> = raw_c
    let p1: ptr<i32> = raw_s
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < 2 * RL3_P2_B {
        bm[i] = 0
        ct[i] = 0
        i = i + 1
    }
    i = 0
    while i < 2 * RL3_P2_B * 16 {
        p1[i] = -1
        i = i + 1
    }
    return R3P2 { bm: bm, ct: ct, p1: p1 }
}

function r3p2_destroy(p: ptr<R3P2>) {
    free(p[0].bm)
    free(p[0].ct)
    free(p[0].p1)
}

# Word w of a big value: deterministic in (key, w) so verify can regenerate it.
function r3_word(key: i32, w: i32) -> i64 {
    let lo: i64 = hash_u32(key + w * 131, 100) as i64
    let hi: i64 = hash_u32(key + w * 137, 101) as i64
    return (hi << 32) | lo
}

# Value-size draw (per key). ~10% of keys get a CHAINED value (65..2^20 bits;
# the class-1 'huge' ones go up to 2^20 — the design supports 2^32, the demo
# stops at 2^20 to stay fast); the rest are direct (1..64 bits).
function r3_draw(state: ptr<i32>, key: i32, words: ptr<i64>,
                 wcnt_out: ptr<i32>, bits_out: ptr<i32>) {
    let r: i32 = rng_next(state) % 200
    let mut bits: i32 = 0
    if r < 180 {
        bits = 1 + (rng_next(state) % 64)               # 1..64 (direct)
    } else {
        if r < 194 {
            bits = 65 + (rng_next(state) % 960)         # 65..1024 (class 0)
        } else {
            if r < 199 {
                bits = 1025 + (rng_next(state) % 64512) # 1025..2^16
            } else {
                bits = 65537 + (rng_next(state) % 196607)  # up to 2^18 (huge;
            }                                              #  design width 2^32)
        }
    }
    let wcnt: i32 = (bits + 63) / 64
    let mut w: i32 = 0
    while w < wcnt {
        let mut v: i64 = r3_word(key, w)
        if w == wcnt - 1 {
            v = v & rl_mask(bits - 64 * (wcnt - 1))
        }
        words[w] = v
        w = w + 1
    }
    wcnt_out[0] = wcnt
    bits_out[0] = bits
}

# Allocate a chained value's words into the pool and a level-1 arena slot.
# Returns the 5-bit p₁ (class + choice + slot), or -1 on bucket-full / pool
# exhaustion.
function r3_arena_alloc(a: ptr<R3Arena>, key: i32, cls: i32, words: ptr<i64>,
                        wcnt: i32) -> i32 {
    let b1: i32 = hash_u32(key, 71 + cls) % RL3_ARENA_B
    let b2: i32 = hash_u32(key, 81 + cls) % RL3_ARENA_B
    let mut pick: i32 = b1
    let mut choice: i32 = 0
    if a[0].ct[cls * RL3_ARENA_B + b2] < a[0].ct[cls * RL3_ARENA_B + b1] {
        pick = b2
        choice = 1
    }
    let slot: i32 = find_free_bit(a[0].bm[cls * RL3_ARENA_B + pick], 8)
    if slot < 0 {
        return -1
    }
    let off: i32 = a[0].fill[0]
    if off + wcnt > RL3_POOL_WORDS {
        return -1
    }
    let mut w: i32 = 0
    while w < wcnt {
        a[0].pool[off + w] = words[w]
        w = w + 1
    }
    a[0].fill[0] = off + wcnt
    a[0].bm[cls * RL3_ARENA_B + pick] = a[0].bm[cls * RL3_ARENA_B + pick] | (1 << slot)
    a[0].ct[cls * RL3_ARENA_B + pick] = a[0].ct[cls * RL3_ARENA_B + pick] + 1
    a[0].soff[(cls * RL3_ARENA_B + pick) * 8 + slot] = off
    return (cls << 4) | (choice << 3) | slot
}

# Absolute slot index (into soff) named by p₁ — the key supplies the bucket.
function r3_arena_sidx(a: ptr<R3Arena>, key: i32, p1: i32) -> i32 {
    let cls: i32 = (p1 >> 4) & 1
    let choice: i32 = (p1 >> 3) & 1
    let slot: i32 = p1 & 7
    let mut b: i32 = hash_u32(key, 71 + cls) % RL3_ARENA_B
    if choice == 1 {
        b = hash_u32(key, 81 + cls) % RL3_ARENA_B
    }
    return (cls * RL3_ARENA_B + b) * 8 + slot
}

function r3_arena_free(a: ptr<R3Arena>, key: i32, p1: i32) {
    let cls: i32 = (p1 >> 4) & 1
    let choice: i32 = (p1 >> 3) & 1
    let slot: i32 = p1 & 7
    let mut b: i32 = hash_u32(key, 71 + cls) % RL3_ARENA_B
    if choice == 1 {
        b = hash_u32(key, 81 + cls) % RL3_ARENA_B
    }
    a[0].bm[cls * RL3_ARENA_B + b] = a[0].bm[cls * RL3_ARENA_B + b] & (0 - (1 << slot) - 1)
    a[0].ct[cls * RL3_ARENA_B + b] = a[0].ct[cls * RL3_ARENA_B + b] - 1
}

# Place the 5-bit p₁ into the level-2 table. Returns the 6-bit p₂, or -1.
function r3_p2_alloc(p: ptr<R3P2>, key: i32, cls: i32, p1: i32) -> i32 {
    let b1: i32 = hash_u32(key, 91 + cls) % RL3_P2_B
    let b2: i32 = hash_u32(key, 101 + cls) % RL3_P2_B
    let mut pick: i32 = b1
    let mut choice: i32 = 0
    if p[0].ct[cls * RL3_P2_B + b2] < p[0].ct[cls * RL3_P2_B + b1] {
        pick = b2
        choice = 1
    }
    let slot: i32 = find_free_bit(p[0].bm[cls * RL3_P2_B + pick], 16)
    if slot < 0 {
        return -1
    }
    p[0].bm[cls * RL3_P2_B + pick] = p[0].bm[cls * RL3_P2_B + pick] | (1 << slot)
    p[0].ct[cls * RL3_P2_B + pick] = p[0].ct[cls * RL3_P2_B + pick] + 1
    p[0].p1[(cls * RL3_P2_B + pick) * 16 + slot] = p1
    return (cls << 5) | (choice << 4) | slot
}

# Absolute slot index (into the p1 array) named by p₂.
function r3_p2_sidx(p: ptr<R3P2>, key: i32, p2: i32) -> i32 {
    let cls: i32 = (p2 >> 5) & 1
    let choice: i32 = (p2 >> 4) & 1
    let slot: i32 = p2 & 15
    let mut b: i32 = hash_u32(key, 91 + cls) % RL3_P2_B
    if choice == 1 {
        b = hash_u32(key, 101 + cls) % RL3_P2_B
    }
    return (cls * RL3_P2_B + b) * 16 + slot
}

function r3_p2_free(p: ptr<R3P2>, key: i32, p2: i32) {
    let cls: i32 = (p2 >> 5) & 1
    let choice: i32 = (p2 >> 4) & 1
    let slot: i32 = p2 & 15
    let mut b: i32 = hash_u32(key, 91 + cls) % RL3_P2_B
    if choice == 1 {
        b = hash_u32(key, 101 + cls) % RL3_P2_B
    }
    p[0].bm[cls * RL3_P2_B + b] = p[0].bm[cls * RL3_P2_B + b] & (0 - (1 << slot) - 1)
    p[0].ct[cls * RL3_P2_B + b] = p[0].ct[cls * RL3_P2_B + b] - 1
}

# Full insert through the three-level chain (direct values skip it).
function r3_insert(reg: ptr<Registry>, base: ptr<VDTable>, p2t: ptr<R3P2>,
                   arena: ptr<R3Arena>, words: ptr<i64>, key: i32,
                   r3w: ptr<i32>, r3p1: ptr<i32>, r3p2v: ptr<i32>,
                   state: ptr<i32>, big_out: ptr<i32>, huge_out: ptr<i32>) -> i32 {
    let idx: i32 = reg[0].next_idx[0]
    if idx >= REG_SIZE {
        return 0
    }
    let mut bits: i32 = 0
    let mut wcnt: i32 = 0
    r3_draw(state, key, words, &wcnt, &bits)
    let mut pos: i32 = 0
    let mut tp: i32 = 0
    let mut p1: i32 = -1
    let mut p2: i32 = -1
    if bits <= 64 {
        let v: i64 = r3_word(key, 0) & rl_mask(bits)
        tp = vd_allocate(base, key, v, bits, &pos)
        if tp < 0 {
            return 0
        }
    } else {
        let mut cls: i32 = 0
        if bits > RL3_HUGE {
            cls = 1
        }
        p1 = r3_arena_alloc(arena, key, cls, words, wcnt)
        if p1 < 0 {
            return 0
        }
        p2 = r3_p2_alloc(p2t, key, cls, p1)
        if p2 < 0 {
            r3_arena_free(arena, key, p1)
            return 0
        }
        tp = vd_allocate(base, key, p2 as i64, 6, &pos)
        if tp < 0 {
            r3_p2_free(p2t, key, p2)
            r3_arena_free(arena, key, p1)
            return 0
        }
        tp = (1 << 7) | tp
        big_out[0] = big_out[0] + 1
        if cls == 1 {
            huge_out[0] = huge_out[0] + 1
        }
    }
    reg[0].next_idx[0] = idx + 1
    reg[0].key[idx] = key
    reg[0].tp[idx] = tp
    reg[0].pos[idx] = pos
    reg[0].live[idx] = 1
    r3w[idx] = bits
    r3p1[idx] = p1
    r3p2v[idx] = p2
    let top: i32 = reg[0].live_top[0]
    reg[0].live_list[top] = idx
    reg[0].live_top[0] = top + 1
    return 1
}

function r3_delete_random(reg: ptr<Registry>, base: ptr<VDTable>, p2t: ptr<R3P2>,
                          arena: ptr<R3Arena>, r3p1: ptr<i32>, r3p2v: ptr<i32>,
                          state: ptr<i32>) -> i32 {
    let top: i32 = reg[0].live_top[0]
    if top == 0 {
        return 0
    }
    let j: i32 = rng_next(state) % top
    let idx: i32 = reg[0].live_list[j]
    let key: i32 = reg[0].key[idx]
    let tp: i32 = reg[0].tp[idx]
    if ((tp >> 7) & 1) == 1 {
        r3_p2_free(p2t, key, r3p2v[idx])
        r3_arena_free(arena, key, r3p1[idx])
    }
    vd_free(base, key, tp)
    reg[0].live[idx] = 0
    reg[0].tp[idx] = -1
    reg[0].live_list[j] = reg[0].live_list[top - 1]
    reg[0].live_top[0] = top - 1
    return 1
}

# Verify EVERY live key bit-exactly: direct values compare the one base word;
# chained values regenerate all pool words through base → p₂ → p₁ → pool.
function r3_verify(reg: ptr<Registry>, base: ptr<VDTable>, p2t: ptr<R3P2>,
                   arena: ptr<R3Arena>, r3w: ptr<i32>, r3p1: ptr<i32>,
                   r3p2v: ptr<i32>) -> i32 {
    let mut errors: i32 = 0
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < REG_SIZE {
        if reg[0].live[i] == 1 {
            let key: i32 = reg[0].key[i]
            let tp: i32 = reg[0].tp[i]
            let bits: i32 = r3w[i]
            let wcnt: i32 = (bits + 63) / 64
            let pos: i32 = vd_dereference(base, key, tp)
            if pos != reg[0].pos[i] {
                errors = errors + 1
            }
            if ((tp >> 7) & 1) == 0 {
                if pos == reg[0].pos[i] and base[0].store[pos] != (r3_word(key, 0) & rl_mask(bits)) {
                    errors = errors + 1
                }
                if vd_cap((tp >> 4) & 7) < bits {
                    errors = errors + 1
                }
            } else {
                let p2: i32 = r3p2v[i]
                if ((tp >> 4) & 7) != RL3_PTR_CLASS {
                    errors = errors + 1
                }
                if pos == reg[0].pos[i] and (base[0].store[pos] as i32) != p2 {
                    errors = errors + 1
                }
                let ps: i32 = r3_p2_sidx(p2t, key, p2)
                let p1: i32 = p2t[0].p1[ps]
                if p1 != r3p1[i] {
                    errors = errors + 1
                }
                let as_: i32 = r3_arena_sidx(arena, key, p1)
                let off: i32 = arena[0].soff[as_]
                let mut w: i32 = 0
                while w < wcnt {
                    let mut ew: i64 = r3_word(key, w)
                    if w == wcnt - 1 {
                        ew = ew & rl_mask(bits - 64 * (wcnt - 1))
                    }
                    if arena[0].pool[off + w] != ew {
                        errors = errors + 1
                    }
                    w = w + 1
                }
            }
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return errors
}

# Structural invariants: bucket occupancies, p₁/p₂ widths, pool fill.
function r3_table_check(reg: ptr<Registry>, base: ptr<VDTable>, p2t: ptr<R3P2>,
                        arena: ptr<R3Arena>, r3p1: ptr<i32>, r3p2v: ptr<i32>) -> i32 {
    let mut errs: i32 = 0
    let mut cls: i32 = 0
    while cls < 2 {
        let mut b: i32 = 0
        while b < RL3_ARENA_B {
            if arena[0].ct[cls * RL3_ARENA_B + b] > 8 {
                errs = errs + 1
            }
            b = b + 1
        }
        b = 0
        while b < RL3_P2_B {
            if p2t[0].ct[cls * RL3_P2_B + b] > 16 {
                errs = errs + 1
            }
            b = b + 1
        }
        cls = cls + 1
    }
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < REG_SIZE {
        if reg[0].live[i] == 1 {
            let tp: i32 = reg[0].tp[i]
            if ((tp >> 7) & 1) == 1 {
                if r3p1[i] < 0 or r3p1[i] >= 32 {
                    errs = errs + 1
                }
                if r3p2v[i] < 0 or r3p2v[i] >= 64 {
                    errs = errs + 1
                }
            }
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    if arena[0].fill[0] > RL3_POOL_WORDS {
        errs = errs + 1
    }
    return errs
}

# ============================================================================
# --- Phase 16: optimal internal-memory stash (paper Theorem 10, §6.6) --------
# ============================================================================
#
# The paper's final application: a small INTERNAL stash that tells you where
# each element of a large EXTERNAL structure resides. External memory is a
# dereference table with load factor 1 − ε (ε = 3/4 here): ST_NB buckets of
# ST_B slots, and each key's bucket is IMPLICIT — a pure two-choice hash of
# the key (ST_C1_SALT / ST_C2_SALT). The stash entry therefore only stores the
# intra-bucket address: 1 choice bit + ⌈log₂ ST_B⌉ slot bits = a 4-bit tiny
# pointer = Θ(log ε⁻¹), NOT log₂((1+ε)m) = 15 bits (the naive full-address
# stash). The tiny pointer never grows with the external array size.
#
# Stash lookup is the adaptive filter of Bender et al. [12]: keys are binned
# by their hash QUOTIENT (bin index = quotient, stored nowhere — the filter's
# implicit-quotient trick), and each entry carries a PREFIX-FREE fingerprint
# (top `len` bits of the 20-bit rest) whose length grows only enough to
# separate bin-mates — the paper's adaptivity bits, O(m) total. The invariant:
# for any two entries a, b in one bin, common-prefix(restₐ, rest_b) <
# min(lenₐ, len_b), maintained by a fixpoint on insert (O(1) amortized, the
# paper's 'the filter will sometimes change the lengths of O(1) fingerprints').
# Query(k) scans k's bin for the UNIQUE fingerprint that is a prefix of h(k)
# (at most one can match — two prefixes of the same string are prefixes of
# each other) — exact, O(1) expected RAM — then reads external memory exactly
# once. Deletes never break the invariant. Positions are permanent once placed
# (page-table stability): external slots are never moved, only freed.
# ============================================================================

struct STStash {
    ext: ptr<i32>,        # ST_EXT external slots (-1 = free)
    bin_head: ptr<i32>,   # ST_BINS — head of each bin's entry list
    ent_next: ptr<i32>,   # REG_SIZE — next entry in the same bin
    ent_rest: ptr<i32>,   # REG_SIZE — full fingerprint rest per entry
    ent_len: ptr<i32>,    # REG_SIZE — prefix-free fingerprint length (adaptivity)
    ent_ptr: ptr<i32>,    # REG_SIZE — 4-bit tiny pointer (1 choice + 3 slot)
    reads: ptr<i32>       # external-memory reads (single-access claim)
}

function st_create() -> STStash {
    let ex_raw: ptr<u8> = malloc(ST_EXT * 4)
    let bh_raw: ptr<u8> = malloc(ST_BINS * 4)
    let en_raw: ptr<u8> = malloc(REG_SIZE * 4)
    let er_raw: ptr<u8> = malloc(REG_SIZE * 4)
    let el_raw: ptr<u8> = malloc(REG_SIZE * 4)
    let ep_raw: ptr<u8> = malloc(REG_SIZE * 4)
    let rd_raw: ptr<u8> = malloc(4)
    let ext: ptr<i32> = ex_raw
    let bin_head: ptr<i32> = bh_raw
    let ent_next: ptr<i32> = en_raw
    let ent_rest: ptr<i32> = er_raw
    let ent_len: ptr<i32> = el_raw
    let ent_ptr: ptr<i32> = ep_raw
    let reads: ptr<i32> = rd_raw
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < ST_EXT {
        ext[i] = -1
        i = i + 1
    }
    i = 0
    while i < ST_BINS {
        bin_head[i] = -1
        i = i + 1
    }
    i = 0
    while i < REG_SIZE {
        ent_next[i] = -1
        ent_rest[i] = 0
        ent_len[i] = 0
        ent_ptr[i] = 0
        i = i + 1
    }
    reads[0] = 0
    return STStash {
        ext: ext, bin_head: bin_head, ent_next: ent_next,
        ent_rest: ent_rest, ent_len: ent_len, ent_ptr: ent_ptr,
        reads: reads
    }
}

function st_destroy(st: ptr<STStash>) {
    free(st[0].ext)
    free(st[0].bin_head)
    free(st[0].ent_next)
    free(st[0].ent_rest)
    free(st[0].ent_len)
    free(st[0].ent_ptr)
    free(st[0].reads)
}

function st_min2(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 {
    if a < b {
        return a
    }
    return b
}

# Length of the leading shared bit-prefix of two w-bit fingerprints.
function st_common_prefix(a: i32, b: i32, w: i32) -> i32 {
    let mut x: i32 = a ^ b
    let mut n: i32 = 0
    while n < w {
        let bit: i32 = (x >> (w - 1 - n)) & 1
        if bit != 0 {
            return n
        }
        n = n + 1
    }
    return w
}

# Place the value in external memory: stable two-choice, first free slot of
# the less-occupied-looking candidate. Returns the absolute slot, or -1 if
# both buckets are full (should be ~never at load 1/4 with 8 slots).
function st_place(st: ptr<STStash>, key: i32, value: i32) -> i32 {
    let c1: i32 = hash_u32(key, ST_C1_SALT) % ST_NB
    let c2: i32 = hash_u32(key, ST_C2_SALT) % ST_NB
    let base1: i32 = c1 * ST_B
    let mut s: i32 = 0
    while s < ST_B {
        if st[0].ext[base1 + s] == -1 {
            st[0].ext[base1 + s] = value
            return base1 + s
        }
        s = s + 1
    }
    let base2: i32 = c2 * ST_B
    s = 0
    while s < ST_B {
        if st[0].ext[base2 + s] == -1 {
            st[0].ext[base2 + s] = value
            return base2 + s
        }
        s = s + 1
    }
    return -1
}

# Restore the prefix-free invariant in a whole bin (the paper's adaptivity):
# repeatedly extend the shorter fingerprint of any colliding PAIR until no pair
# shares ≥ min(len) bits. Pair-wise, not just new-vs-mates — extending one
# entry can break another pair, and the fixpoint must catch that. The bin is
# tiny (avg m/ST_BINS ≈ 4 entries), so the O(b²) scan is constant expected.
# Returns 0 only on a true fingerprint collision (two identical rests, which
# the demo's key set never produces — the 31-bit salted hash is injective on
# the 2^24-key domain).
function st_fp_fix(st: ptr<STStash>, bin: i32) -> i32 {
    let w: i32 = 31 - ST_Q_BITS
    let mut changed: i32 = 1
    while changed == 1 {
        changed = 0
        let mut a: i32 = st[0].bin_head[bin]
        while a >= 0 {
            let mut b: i32 = st[0].ent_next[a]
            while b >= 0 {
                let sp: i32 = st_common_prefix(st[0].ent_rest[a], st[0].ent_rest[b], w)
                if sp == w {
                    return 0
                }
                if sp >= st_min2(st[0].ent_len[a], st[0].ent_len[b]) {
                    if st[0].ent_len[a] <= st[0].ent_len[b] {
                        st[0].ent_len[a] = sp + 1
                    } else {
                        st[0].ent_len[b] = sp + 1
                    }
                    changed = 1
                }
                b = st[0].ent_next[b]
            }
            a = st[0].ent_next[a]
        }
    }
    return 1
}

# Full stash lookup: compute the implicit quotient bin, scan the bin for the
# unique fingerprint that is a prefix of h(k) (O(bin) expected O(1) RAM), then
# read external memory EXACTLY ONCE. Returns the external position (-1 = not
# found, which must never happen for a live key).
function st_query(st: ptr<STStash>, key: i32) -> i32 {
    let c1: i32 = hash_u32(key, ST_C1_SALT) % ST_NB
    let c2: i32 = hash_u32(key, ST_C2_SALT) % ST_NB
    let hq: i32 = hash_u32(key, ST_Q_SALT)
    let bin: i32 = hq & (ST_BINS - 1)
    let rest: i32 = hq >> ST_Q_BITS
    let w: i32 = 31 - ST_Q_BITS
    let mut e: i32 = st[0].bin_head[bin]
    while e >= 0 {
        if st_common_prefix(rest, st[0].ent_rest[e], w) >= st[0].ent_len[e] {
            let tp: i32 = st[0].ent_ptr[e]
            let choice: i32 = (tp >> 3) & 1
            let slot: i32 = tp & 7
            st[0].reads[0] = st[0].reads[0] + 1
            if choice == 0 {
                return c1 * ST_B + slot
            }
            return c2 * ST_B + slot
        }
        e = st[0].ent_next[e]
    }
    return -1
}

function st_unlink(st: ptr<STStash>, bin: i32, idx: i32) {
    let mut e: i32 = st[0].bin_head[bin]
    let mut prev: i32 = -1
    while e >= 0 {
        if e == idx {
            if prev >= 0 {
                st[0].ent_next[prev] = st[0].ent_next[e]
            } else {
                st[0].bin_head[bin] = st[0].ent_next[e]
            }
            return
        }
        prev = e
        e = st[0].ent_next[e]
    }
}

function st_insert(reg: ptr<Registry>, st: ptr<STStash>, key: i32, value: i32) -> i32 {
    let idx: i32 = reg[0].next_idx[0]
    if idx >= REG_SIZE {
        return 0
    }
    let pos: i32 = st_place(st, key, value)
    if pos < 0 {
        return 0
    }
    let c1: i32 = hash_u32(key, ST_C1_SALT) % ST_NB
    let c2: i32 = hash_u32(key, ST_C2_SALT) % ST_NB
    let base1: i32 = c1 * ST_B
    let base2: i32 = c2 * ST_B
    let mut choice: i32 = 0
    let mut base: i32 = base1
    if pos >= base1 and pos < base1 + ST_B {
        choice = 0
        base = base1
    } else {
        choice = 1
        base = base2
    }
    let slot: i32 = pos - base
    let tp: i32 = (choice << 3) | slot
    let hq: i32 = hash_u32(key, ST_Q_SALT)
    let bin: i32 = hq & (ST_BINS - 1)
    let rest: i32 = hq >> ST_Q_BITS
    st[0].ent_rest[idx] = rest
    st[0].ent_len[idx] = 1
    st[0].ent_ptr[idx] = tp
    st[0].ent_next[idx] = st[0].bin_head[bin]
    st[0].bin_head[bin] = idx
    let fixok: i32 = st_fp_fix(st, bin)
    if fixok == 0 {
        st_unlink(st, bin, idx)
        st[0].ext[pos] = -1
        return 0
    }
    reg[0].next_idx[0] = idx + 1
    reg[0].key[idx] = key
    reg[0].val[idx] = value
    reg[0].tp[idx] = tp
    reg[0].pos[idx] = pos
    reg[0].live[idx] = 1
    let top: i32 = reg[0].live_top[0]
    reg[0].live_list[top] = idx
    reg[0].live_top[0] = top + 1
    return 1
}

function st_delete_random(reg: ptr<Registry>, st: ptr<STStash>, state: ptr<i32>) -> i32 {
    let top: i32 = reg[0].live_top[0]
    if top == 0 {
        return 0
    }
    let j: i32 = rng_next(state) % top
    let idx: i32 = reg[0].live_list[j]
    let key: i32 = reg[0].key[idx]
    let pos: i32 = reg[0].pos[idx]
    st[0].ext[pos] = -1                      # free the slot — others never move
    let hq: i32 = hash_u32(key, ST_Q_SALT)
    let bin: i32 = hq & (ST_BINS - 1)
    st_unlink(st, bin, idx)
    reg[0].live[idx] = 0
    reg[0].tp[idx] = -1
    reg[0].live_list[j] = reg[0].live_list[top - 1]
    reg[0].live_top[0] = top - 1
    return 1
}

# Re-derive EVERY live key's position through the stash (bin scan + one
# external read) and compare against the recorded position and value.
function st_verify(reg: ptr<Registry>, st: ptr<STStash>) -> i32 {
    let mut errors: i32 = 0
    let mut i: i32 = 0
    while i < REG_SIZE {
        if reg[0].live[i] == 1 {
            let key: i32 = reg[0].key[i]
            let pos: i32 = st_query(st, key)
            if pos != reg[0].pos[i] {
                errors = errors + 1
            } else {
                if st[0].ext[pos] != reg[0].val[i] {
                    errors = errors + 1
                }
            }
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return errors
}

# Structural invariants: external bucket occupancy (≤ ST_B, total == live) and
# the prefix-free fingerprint invariant in every stash bin.
function sttable_check(reg: ptr<Registry>, st: ptr<STStash>) -> i32 {
    let mut errs: i32 = 0
    let w: i32 = 31 - ST_Q_BITS
    let mut b: i32 = 0
    let mut occupied: i32 = 0
    while b < ST_NB {
        let mut occ: i32 = 0
        let mut s: i32 = 0
        while s < ST_B {
            if st[0].ext[b * ST_B + s] != -1 {
                occ = occ + 1
                occupied = occupied + 1
            }
            s = s + 1
        }
        if occ > ST_B {
            errs = errs + 1
        }
        b = b + 1
    }
    if occupied != reg[0].live_top[0] {
        errs = errs + 1
    }
    b = 0
    while b < ST_BINS {
        let mut e1: i32 = st[0].bin_head[b]
        while e1 >= 0 {
            let mut e2: i32 = st[0].ent_next[e1]
            while e2 >= 0 {
                let sp: i32 = st_common_prefix(st[0].ent_rest[e1], st[0].ent_rest[e2], w)
                if sp >= st_min2(st[0].ent_len[e1], st[0].ent_len[e2]) {
                    errs = errs + 1
                }
                e2 = st[0].ent_next[e2]
            }
            if st[0].ent_len[e1] < 1 or st[0].ent_len[e1] > w {
                errs = errs + 1
            }
            if st[0].ent_ptr[e1] < 0 or st[0].ent_ptr[e1] >= 16 {
                errs = errs + 1
            }
            e1 = st[0].ent_next[e1]
        }
        b = b + 1
    }
    return errs
}

